var Q_OPHTHO_EP2 = [
{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-7", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-intro-exam",
  stem:"Action of the superior oblique muscle:",
  options:["elevation in abduction","Elevation in adduction","Depression in abduction","Depression in adduction"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"The superior oblique is tested with the eye adducted, where its primary field of action is depression; it is supplied by the trochlear nerve (cranial nerve IV). The lecture's own muscle-action table (L1,2, slide 69) lists the superior oblique's primary anatomical action as intorsion, with depression and abduction as secondary and tertiary effects -- the \"depression in adduction\" phrasing used here describes the field of action tested clinically (the direction of gaze in which the muscle's vertical effect is greatest), which is the standard clinical convention rather than a direct restatement of the table's own columns (not taken from the course material).\n\nOption A, elevation in abduction, is the field of action of the superior rectus, which the same table lists with elevation as its primary action and adduction as its tertiary action -- abduction is not one of its listed columns, so this option combines the wrong muscle with the wrong position. Option B, elevation in adduction, belongs to the inferior oblique, whose primary action is extorsion with elevation as a secondary effect; adduction is not one of its listed columns either, so again the position given does not match this muscle's own table row. Option C, depression in abduction, belongs to the inferior rectus, whose primary action is depression with extorsion secondary and adduction tertiary; abduction is not printed as one of its own action columns, so it is the wrong pairing of muscle and position as well.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The superior oblique muscle primarily causes intorsion and depression when the eye is adducted. It’s supplied by the trochlear nerve (CN IV).\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"The superior oblique muscle's tested field of action is depression in adduction, and it is supplied by the trochlear nerve.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1404-1405" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-25", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-red",
  stem:"Red eye is seen in all EXCEPT:",
  options:["Corneal ulcers","Conjunctivitis","Iridocyclitis","Primary open angle glaucoma"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Primary open-angle glaucoma is the classic painless, symptom-poor disease that damages the optic nerve over years without producing external inflammation, so the eye stays white; this is why it is picked out from the other three as the exception. The lecture material on red eye states this directly (L37, slide 12): \"the more common chronic open-angle glaucoma causes no redness of the eye,\" contrasting it with acute angle-closure glaucoma, which does present as a painful red eye (this is a different, uncommon form of glaucoma from the one named in the stem).\n\nCorneal ulcers, conjunctivitis and iridocyclitis are all inflammatory or infective surface/anterior-segment conditions and all produce visible redness -- ulcers and iridocyclitis typically as a circumcorneal (ciliary) flush and conjunctivitis as a more diffuse conjunctival injection; the source material's own differential table for the red eye lists acute iridocyclitis, keratitis, bacterial conjunctivitis, viral conjunctivitis and allergic conjunctivitis side by side as red-eye conditions, with open-angle glaucoma notably absent from that table (open-angle glaucoma not being a red-eye condition at all).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Primary open-angle glaucoma causes painless, gradual vision loss without redness. In contrast, corneal ulcers, conjunctivitis, and iridocyclitis all present with red eyes.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Primary open-angle glaucoma is a painless, white-eye disease and is the exception among red-eye conditions such as corneal ulcers, conjunctivitis and iridocyclitis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1440-1441" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-27", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-lac",
  stem:"Acute dacryocystitis is treated by:",
  options:["Antibiotics and hot fomentation","Dacryocystorhinostomy","Probing","Dacryocystectomy"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"The lacrimal system lecture gives acute dacryocystitis its own treatment heading (L9, slide 32), and it opens with exactly this: \"initial treatment involves the application of warm compresses and oral antibiotics,\" with the same slide going on to say that irrigation and probing should not be performed during the acute phase, and that incision and drainage is reserved for a pointing abscess.\n\nOption B, dacryocystorhinostomy, is the definitive surgical treatment named on the same slide, but it is explicitly positioned as something \"commonly required after the acute infection has been controlled\" -- i.e. a later, elective step, not the acute-phase treatment the stem asks for. Option C, probing, is the one intervention the lecture names as contraindicated at this stage (\"irrigation and probing should not be performed\"), because instrumenting an acutely infected, tense lacrimal sac risks rupturing it or spreading the infection. Option D, dacryocystectomy (excision of the lacrimal sac) is not mentioned anywhere in this slide's treatment section for either the acute or the chronic form; it is a salvage procedure used in specific circumstances such as a failed DCR or where the sac itself must be removed, and is not part of the standard first-line management the lecture describes (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Acute dacryocystitis is an infection of the lacrimal sac and is treated initially with systemic antibiotics and warm compresses. Surgical procedures like DCR are reserved for chronic cases after the acute phase resolves.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Acute dacryocystitis is treated first with warm compresses and oral antibiotics; probing is avoided in the acute phase and DCR is deferred until the infection is controlled.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1444-1445" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-32", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"Diurnal variation in ptosis is seen in:",
  options:["Congenital ptosis","Aponeurotic ptosis","Traumatic ptosis","Myasthenia gravis"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disease of the neuromuscular junction, and the muscle weakness it causes -- including ptosis of the levator palpebrae superioris -- is fatigable: it worsens with sustained use of the muscle over the course of the day and improves with rest, which is what produces the diurnal (day-to-day, worse-by-evening) pattern the stem describes (not taken from the course material, though the mechanism underlying this pattern is standard). The eyelid disease lecture's slide on myogenic (myasthenic) ptosis (L5, slide 13) illustrates exactly this variability with a before/after photograph pair of the same patient (one with ptosis, one after rest, now symmetrical), plus a separate pair showing the lid drooping again after sustained upgaze -- a direct visual demonstration of fatigability, alongside labelled photographs of the ice-pack test and the Tensilon test, both of which transiently reverse the ptosis and support the myasthenic diagnosis.\n\nCongenital ptosis is a fixed developmental defect of the levator muscle or its innervation and does not fluctuate through the day. Aponeurotic (involutional/senile) ptosis results from stretching or dehiscence of the levator aponeurosis with age and is likewise a fixed mechanical deficit, not a fatigable one. Traumatic ptosis follows direct injury to the lid, levator or its nerve supply and is also a fixed, structural problem rather than one that varies with the time of day (all three not taken from the course material, though the point that they are not fatigable is standard clinical teaching).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In myasthenia gravis, ptosis worsens as the day progresses due to fatigable muscle weakness. This diurnal variation is a key diagnostic clue.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Myasthenia gravis causes a fatigable ptosis that worsens through the day, unlike the fixed ptosis of congenital, aponeurotic or traumatic causes.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1454-1455" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-34", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"Which of the following is a part of the uveal tract?",
  options:["The ciliary body","The limbus","The cornea","The retina"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"The uvea & sclera lecture opens its uveal tract slide (L13,14, slide 3) with the tract broken into exactly three parts: iris, ciliary body (with its pars plicata and pars plana) and choroid, alongside a labelled cross-section diagram that picks the uveal tract out in red against the cornea, lens, vitreous, retina and sclera drawn around it. The ciliary body is one of those three named parts.\n\nOption B, the limbus, is the anatomical junction between the cornea and the sclera/conjunctiva -- it is a landmark on the ocular surface, not a layer of the vascular coat, and it is not listed among the uvea's parts on this slide. Option C, the cornea, and option D, the retina, are both drawn on the same cross-section diagram as separate, clearly distinct structures from the uveal tract picked out in red -- the cornea is the avascular anterior refractive window and the retina is the innermost neurosensory layer, and neither is one of the tract's three named components.\n\n**A live near-sibling entry, `ophep-uveal-tract-4`, asks the same underlying fact with a different distractor set (lens, sclera, cornea).**\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The uveal tract includes the iris, ciliary body, and choroid, which are the vascular middle layers of the eye. The cornea and retina are not part of the uveal tract.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"The uveal tract is made up of the iris, ciliary body and choroid; the limbus, cornea and retina are separate structures.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1458-1459" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-35", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro-optic",
  stem:"Which of the following can cause painless loss of vision?",
  options:["Acute glaucoma","Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy","Corneal abrasion","Conjunctivitis"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (AION) is caused by infarction of the optic nerve head and typically presents as sudden, painless visual loss; the optic nerve lecture's AION slide (L21, slide 9) shows this with a fundus photograph of a swollen, pale disc with a blurred margin and an obliterated cup, together with a visual field printout showing a dense altitudinal (superior-half) defect -- the classic AION picture -- though the word \"painless\" itself is not printed on that slide (not taken from the course material, though it is standard clinical teaching for AION).\n\nThe acute visual loss lecture separately sets up a painful/painless classification of sudden vision loss, but its own author never populates that classification with which named conditions fall on which side, and states that painful causes are represented by no case at all in the deck. Acute (angle-closure) glaucoma's pain is printed directly in its own lecture: the Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma symptoms slide lists \"Pain -> ++++\" as the first symptom (L12, slide 19), alongside coloured haloes, rapid diminution of vision, redness and nausea/vomiting -- a painful presentation, unlike the painless AION in the correct answer. The claim that corneal abrasion belongs on the \"painful\" side of this grouping rests on general clinical knowledge, not on anything printed in either source (not taken from the course material). Corneal abrasion is a surface epithelial defect that is intensely painful because the cornea is one of the most densely innervated tissues in the body, and it does not typically cause a marked drop in vision unless it is extensive or centrally located. Conjunctivitis is a surface inflammatory condition that classically spares vision altogether (aside from mild blurring from discharge) and is not a cause of true visual loss at all.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (AION) presents with sudden, painless vision loss due to infarction of the optic nerve head. Other options like acute glaucoma or corneal abrasion are typically painful.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy causes sudden painless visual loss with a swollen pale disc, unlike the painful presentations of acute glaucoma and corneal abrasion or the vision-sparing picture of conjunctivitis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1460-1461" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-46", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"Which of the following can cause miosis?",
  options:["Acute angle closure glaucoma","Acute iridocyclitis","Acute conjunctivitis","Allergic conjunctivitis"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The uvea & sclera lecture's slide on the signs of anterior uveitis (iridocyclitis) (L13,14, slide 17) lists the pupil sign directly: \"miosis with sluggish pupillary light reaction,\" together with an irregular pupil shape from synechiae -- the sphincter pupillae is irritated by the inflamed iris, producing a small, poorly reactive pupil, and the same slide's photograph shows a small, slightly irregular pupil in an inflamed eye.\n\nAcute angle-closure glaucoma produces the opposite pupillary sign: its own Signs slide (L12, slide 20) lists a \"mid-dilated vertically oval non-reactive pupil\" among the acute-angle-closure signs, the result of ischaemic paralysis of the iris sphincter from the sudden pressure rise (the ischaemic-paralysis mechanism itself is not taken from the course material, though it is standard clinical teaching and is the recognised contrast with iridocyclitis's miosis). Acute conjunctivitis is a surface inflammation of the conjunctiva that does not involve the iris or its sphincter muscle at all, so it does not alter pupil size. Allergic conjunctivitis is likewise a surface hypersensitivity reaction confined to the conjunctiva, without any effect on the pupil.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Miosis occurs in acute iridocyclitis due to irritation of the sphincter pupillae muscle. Other signs include ciliary injection and pain.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Acute iridocyclitis causes miosis with a sluggish light reaction from sphincter irritation, unlike the mid-dilated pupil of acute angle-closure glaucoma or the uninvolved pupil in conjunctivitis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1482-1483" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-50", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-refract",
  stem:"Type of staphyloma in high myopia is:",
  options:["Anterior staphyloma","Ciliary staphyloma","Intercalary staphyloma","Posterior staphyloma"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"The refractive errors lecture (L7,8, slide 46) lists posterior staphyloma as one of the named complications of high myopia, alongside chorioretinal degenerations, complicated cataract and a high association with primary open-angle glaucoma -- reflecting the progressive stretching and thinning of the posterior sclera around the optic disc and macula that occurs as the myopic eye elongates.\n\nAnterior, ciliary and intercalary staphyloma are all real staphyloma types classified by the site of the scleral outpouching (anterior, at the ciliary body region, and at the limbus/intercalary zone respectively), but they are associated with other causes of scleral thinning and ectasia -- such as previous inflammation, trauma or long-standing glaucoma -- rather than with the posterior elongation of high myopia; none of these three site-based categories is named on the myopia complications slide, so their distinction from posterior staphyloma is general ophthalmic knowledge rather than a fact printed in this lecture (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In high myopia, stretching of the posterior sclera causes a posterior staphyloma near the optic disc. It leads to progressive visual deterioration.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"High myopia produces a posterior staphyloma from progressive stretching of the sclera around the disc and macula, distinct from the anterior, ciliary and intercalary types seen with other causes of scleral ectasia.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1490-1491" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-58", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-intro-exam",
  stem:"Color vision is tested by:",
  options:["Hess screen","Ishihara plates","Lancaster screen","Amsler's grid"],
  answer:1,
  image:"q-oph-ep-1506", imgAlt:"Circular plate made of many coloured dots of varying size",
  explanation:"The basic eye examination lecture lists colour vision testing as one of the complementary tests on its examination-summary slide (L1,2, slide 103), and Ishihara pseudoisochromatic plates -- coloured dot patterns that spell out numbers or shapes visible only to eyes with normal red-green colour discrimination -- are the standard clinical tool used for this purpose, though the lecture's summary slide names the test category without printing the word \"Ishihara\" itself (not taken from the course material for the specific plate name, though it is the universally used test and is exactly what the endpoint box below names).\n\nThe Hess screen and Lancaster screen are both used to chart and quantify ocular misalignment and diplopia from extraocular muscle imbalance (for example in cranial nerve palsies or thyroid eye disease) by plotting where each eye projects a target under red/green dissociation -- they assess motility, not colour perception, and neither is named in this lecture (not taken from the course material). The Amsler grid is a different complementary test on the same examination summary, used to assess the central 10 degrees of the visual field for metamorphopsia or scotomas, most often in macular disease -- it tests central field integrity, not colour discrimination.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Ishihara plates are used to assess color vision, mainly detecting red-green color deficiencies. They consist of colored dot patterns forming numbers or shapes.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Ishihara plates test colour vision, distinct from the Hess and Lancaster screens (motility/diplopia charting) and the Amsler grid (central field testing).",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1506-1507" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-73", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-conj",
  stem:"Recurrent pterygium may be treated by:",
  options:["Simple excision","Follow up","Excision with conjunctival autograft","Topical steroids followed by excision"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"The conjunctiva lecture shows an operative photograph of pterygium excision with a sutured graft on its final pterygium slide (L6, slide 57), which is the only sourced anchor for surgical management in this deck -- the lecture never prints the words \"treatment,\" \"management,\" \"indication\" or a recurrence rate anywhere in its pterygium slides. Excision with conjunctival autograft covering the bare sclera is the standard surgical technique used clinically to reduce the risk of recurrence, since the graft restores a normal conjunctival surface over the excision bed and limits regrowth of fibrovascular tissue toward the cornea (not taken from the course material).\n\nSimple excision without a graft (the \"bare sclera\" technique) is the option this lecture's own photograph implicitly moves away from, and it is well recognised to carry a substantially higher recurrence rate than excision with grafting, which is precisely why a RECURRENT pterygium (one that has already failed a prior excision) is managed with the more definitive grafting approach rather than being re-excised alone (not taken from the course material). Follow-up alone is not treatment at all and does not address an already-recurrent lesion that is presumably growing and symptomatic. Topical steroids followed by excision is not a recognised staged protocol for pterygium in the source material or in general ophthalmic teaching; steroids may be used briefly to settle inflammation before elective surgery but are not a treatment sequence specific to recurrence, and grafting at the time of excision, not steroid pre-treatment, is what is understood to lower the chance of a further recurrence (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Recurrent pterygium is best managed by excision followed by conjunctival autograft to reduce recurrence risk.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recurrent pterygium is treated by excision with conjunctival autograft, which lowers recurrence risk compared with simple bare-sclera excision.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1536-1537" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-3-43", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-peds",
  stem:"Even if retinopathy of prematurity is successfully-treated, those infants still have a risk to develop which of the following conditions?",
  options:["Hyperopia","Myopia","Amblyopia","All of the above"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Retinopathy of prematurity (ROP) is a proliferative retinopathy of preterm, low-birth-weight infants that ends in epiretinal fibrosis and tractional changes if untreated (L33,34, slide 19). Even after treatment succeeds in stopping the proliferative disease, treated ROP eyes carry a lasting higher rate of myopia than the general infant population -- refractive change is one of its long-term sequelae, distinct from the retinal-detachment risk that marks the untreated, late-stage disease (not taken from the course material, which does not discuss ROP's post-treatment refractive outcome).\n\nOption A, hyperopia, is the wrong direction of refractive shift -- ROP-associated refractive error runs myopic, not hyperopic, most likely from the altered anterior-segment and axial growth that accompanies the peripheral retinal disease. Option C, amblyopia, can certainly follow severe ROP, but usually as a consequence of a structural complication (macular dragging, retinal detachment, or the anisometropia produced by asymmetric disease) rather than as the direct, well-known residual risk after otherwise-successful treatment that this stem is asking about; it is not the association the question is built on. Option D, \"all of the above\", is therefore wrong because hyperopia is not part of the picture, so the combination cannot be correct.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Prognosis of retinopathy of prematurity:\n> 🔴 Higher incidence of myopia among ROP infants, even if treated successfully.\n> 🔴 If treated early, the prognosis is good.\n> 🔴 Late stages with retinal detachment are associated with poor prognosis.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Treated retinopathy of prematurity still carries a raised long-term risk of myopia, distinct from the retinal-detachment risk of untreated, late-stage disease.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1787-1788" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-3-45", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-insid",
  stem:"Which of the following is a simple test that can be used to monitor AMD?",
  options:["Flouresceine angiography","Amsler grid","OCT","Ophthalmoscopy"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The Amsler grid is a grid of straight lines with a central fixation dot that a patient can use at home, one eye at a time, to check for distortion (metamorphopsia) or missing areas within the central 10 degrees of the visual field -- exactly the field the anatomy-and-examination lecture assigns to it (L1,2, slide 123). Because age-related macular degeneration damages central, not peripheral, vision, a test built around fixation and the central field is well suited to catching a new bend in the lines or a new blank patch that signals disease progression; the lecture lists the Amsler grid among the tests of macular function in macular degeneration (L35,36, slide 38); that the patient performs it at home, daily, as a self-monitoring tool is not spelled out in the cached lecture material (not taken from the course material).\n\nThe other three options are all real tests used in macular degeneration but fail the word \"simple\" in the stem: fluorescein angiography is the instrument-based test the chronic-visual-loss lecture lists for macular function, alongside visual acuity, pupillary light reaction, colour vision, ophthalmoscopy, the photostress test and laser interferometry (L35,36, slide 38), and OCT is a clinic imaging test the lecture does not list there (not taken from the course material) -- neither is a test a patient performs alone. Ophthalmoscopy is likewise a clinician's examination technique, not something the patient can do for ongoing self-monitoring between visits. Each of the three requires equipment or a trained examiner, which is what sets the take-home Amsler grid apart as the answer the stem is looking for.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Amsler grid → A simple test that the patient can use every day at home to check and monitor the presence or progression of AMD.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"The Amsler grid is a simple, patient-administered home test of the central 10 degrees of vision, used to monitor for progression of macular disease such as AMD.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1791-1792" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-3-58", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro-optic",
  stem:"A 55-year-old male patient complains of severe headache combined with tinnitus. He reports that the condition developed over a period of few weeks. Fundoscopic examination revealed evident papilledema. Which of the following is the best next step to be done in order to properly manage that patient?",
  options:["Prescribe symptomatic therapeutic preparations","Advise the patient to rest and avoid stress","Orbit ultrasonography","MRI brain"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Papilledema is bilateral disc swelling from raised intracranial pressure (ICP) -- the optic-nerve lecture's own differential table gives papilledema the single listed etiology \"↑ ICP\" (L21, slide 8). A headache that has been building over weeks, together with objective papilledema on fundoscopy, is a red flag for a mass or other intracranial process raising pressure on the optic nerve heads, so the priority is neuroimaging of the brain (MRI) to find the cause before anything else is done; the lecture itself flags that it never lists what actually raises ICP (no tumour, no idiopathic intracranial hypertension, no venous sinus thrombosis, no hydrocephalus are named), so the specific step of ordering MRI brain, and the tinnitus detail pointing toward a pulsatile-tinnitus pattern, are not spelled out in the cached material (not taken from the course material).\n\nOption A, symptomatic therapy, and option B, rest and stress avoidance, both treat this as a benign tension-type headache and would delay the workup of a potentially dangerous, pressure-raising intracranial lesion -- exactly the wrong response to papilledema. Option C, orbit ultrasonography, images the globe and orbit, not the brain or its ventricles/venous sinuses, so it cannot identify a mass, hydrocephalus or venous sinus thrombosis raising ICP; it is the wrong imaging modality for this presentation, even though ultrasonography does have a role elsewhere in ophthalmology (e.g. in globe trauma).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Headache of insidious onset and progressive course + papilledema → elevated intracranial pressure → MRI brain.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Progressive headache with papilledema signals raised intracranial pressure and requires MRI brain to find the cause, not symptomatic treatment or orbital imaging.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1817-1818" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-3-59", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-dr",
  stem:"Which of the following is a well-known cause of diminished visual capability in a diabetic patient?",
  options:["Macular edema","Exudative retinal detachment","Posterior vitreous detachment","All of the above"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Diabetic macular oedema is one of the staged categories in the diabetic-retinopathy lecture's own classification of the disease, alongside focal, diffuse and ischaemic patterns and proliferative disease (L19.1, slide 2), and its \"clinically significant\" form is defined by retinal oedema or hard exudates within 500 µm of the centre of the fovea (L19.1, slide 11), while the diffuse pattern is the one the deck marks with variable impairment of visual acuity and a guarded prognosis (L19.1, slide 13). Macular oedema is fluid accumulation at the macula -- the part of the retina responsible for fine, central vision -- so its presence directly reduces visual acuity, and it is one of the disease's most frequently cited causes of vision loss in diabetic patients; the specific ranking of macular oedema as the single most common cause of vision loss in diabetic retinopathy is not stated in the cached lecture material (not taken from the course material), though its listing as a vision-threatening stage of the disease is.\n\nOption B, exudative retinal detachment, is not a typical feature of diabetic eye disease -- diabetic retinopathy instead causes tractional retinal detachment from fibrovascular proliferation and traction, not the exudative (fluid-driven) type, which is more characteristic of conditions such as choroidal tumours or severe uveitis. Option C, posterior vitreous detachment, is an extremely common age-related event that is not itself a diabetes-specific cause of vision loss, and can occur in non-diabetic eyes as well, so it does not answer the stem's premise of a diabetes-specific mechanism. Because B and C are each a poor fit, option D, \"all of the above\", cannot be correct either.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Causes of loss/diminution of vision in DR are:\n> A. In any stage of DR:\n> i. Macular oedema (The most common cause of vision loss in patients with non-proliferative DR)\n> ii. Macular ischemia\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Macular oedema is a staged, vision-threatening feature of diabetic retinopathy and a well-known cause of diminished vision in diabetic patients.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1819-1820" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-3-61", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-vasc",
  stem:"An old diabetic & hypertensive male patient was found to have extensive retinal hemorrhages and cotton wool spots. Which of the following is an expected complication in that patient?",
  options:["Neovascularization in the iris","Neovascularization the choroid","Cataract","All of the above"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Extensive retinal haemorrhages together with variable cotton-wool spots and severe disc oedema are exactly the printed sign list for the ischaemic form of central retinal vein occlusion (L19.2, slide 12), a condition an old diabetic and hypertensive patient is at particular risk for from combined vascular disease. The same slide lists rubeosis iridis -- new vessel growth on the iris -- occurring in about half of these ischaemic cases, which is the ophthalmic name for iris neovascularization; this makes it the expected complication of this clinical picture, and it is the mechanism behind the sight-threatening neovascular glaucoma that ischaemic CRVO can progress to if untreated.\n\nOption B, neovascularization of the choroid, describes choroidal neovascularization, the process that drives wet age-related macular degeneration, not the retinal vein occlusion this vignette describes; it is a different vascular bed and a different disease altogether. Option C, cataract, is an unrelated, much slower lens change that is not a recognised direct complication of an ischaemic vein occlusion in the way iris neovascularization is. Because B and C do not fit this vignette's mechanism, option D, \"all of the above\", is also incorrect.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> CRVO may be complicated by Secondary glaucoma (due to neovascularization of the retina and iris), Vitreous hemorrhage, Macular edema.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Extensive retinal haemorrhages with cotton-wool spots describe ischaemic central retinal vein occlusion, whose expected complication is iris neovascularization (rubeosis iridis), which can progress to secondary (neovascular) glaucoma.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1823-1824" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-3-64", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-squint",
  stem:"What is the age after which treatment of amblyopia is less likely to be successful?",
  options:["4-2 years","6-4 years","10-8 years","15-12 years"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Amblyopia is one of the two sensory adaptations to squint named in the strabismus lecture (suppression and amblyopia), whose treatment by occlusion (patching) is also shown in the same deck (L29, slides 22-23 and 32) -- but the deck's own review notes that no acuity threshold, no degree and no age limit for successful treatment is ever printed on either slide (L29). The 8-10-year cutoff given here, after which the visual system's plasticity has largely closed and occlusion or penalisation therapy becomes far less effective, is standard clinical teaching for amblyopia but is not stated in the cached lecture material (not taken from the course material).\n\nThe House bank prints the same question with the same key.\n\nOptions A (4-2 years), B (6-4 years) and D (15-12 years) all give age ranges that do not match the commonly taught 8-10-year plasticity cutoff for amblyopia treatment: A and B are too young, well within the window where treatment is still expected to succeed, and D is implausibly late for meaningful visual plasticity to still be present, making 10-8 the only range consistent with standard teaching on the closing of the amblyogenic sensitive period.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Amblyopia treatment is less successful after age 8-10 years.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Amblyopia treatment becomes markedly less successful once a child is past roughly 8-10 years of age, as the visual system's plasticity narrows; the lecture teaches amblyopia and its occlusion treatment but gives no age limit itself.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1829-1830" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-3-70", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-vasc",
  stem:"A 65-year-old man presents with sudden painless severe monocular vision loss. Ophthalmoscopic examination revealed cherry red spots together with venous stasis. Which of the following is a medication prescribed by the ophthalmologist in order to control that acute condition?",
  options:["IV mannitol 20%","Systemic steroids","Topical miotics","All of the above"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Sudden, painless, severe monocular vision loss with a cherry-red spot at the macula and marked retinal changes is the classic picture of central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO), printed with the same cherry-red-spot sign in the retinal vascular disorders lecture (L19.2, slide 26); \"venous stasis\" here reflects the sludged, segmented (cattle-trucking) blood column the same slide shows in the affected retinal vessels. Intravenous mannitol is an osmotic diuretic that rapidly lowers intraocular pressure; in CRAO, lowering IOP acutely is one of the emergency measures aimed at dislodging the occluding embolus/thrombus and improving retinal perfusion during the brief window before the retina is irreversibly infarcted. The acute visual loss lecture's own CRAO case leaves its entire management slide blank, naming ocular massage, anterior chamber paracentesis, IOP-lowering drops or acetazolamide, and carbogen/rebreathing as measures that are absent from the deck (L31,32) -- so IV mannitol as a specific emergency IOP-lowering agent for CRAO is not stated in the cached lecture material (not taken from the course material), even though the underlying diagnosis is.\n\nOption B, systemic steroids, is the acute treatment for a different disease process, such as optic neuritis or giant cell arteritis-related ischaemic optic neuropathy, not a vascular occlusion of the retinal circulation. Option C, topical miotics, constrict the pupil and are used in angle-closure glaucoma, not in an arterial occlusion, and would do nothing to improve retinal perfusion. Since B and C do not treat CRAO, option D, \"all of the above\", is also wrong.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Vigorous measures done by the ophthalmologist to lower IOP in case of CRAO → IOP-lowering medications (including intravenous mannitol), AC paracentesis & vasodilators.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Central retinal artery occlusion presents with sudden painless monocular vision loss and a cherry-red spot, and its emergency management includes IOP-lowering measures such as IV mannitol, aimed at restoring retinal perfusion.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1841-1842" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-3-77", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro-motil",
  stem:"A 32-year-old woman sees an ophthalmologist as she suffers from eyelid drooping together with eye deviation outwards and downwards. Examination revealed unilateral mydriasis. Which of the following nerve paralysis is the cause of the patient’s manifestations?",
  options:["Oculomotor nerve","Trochlear nerve","Abducent nerve","Facial nerve"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"The eyelid disease lecture shows exactly this triad on one slide: complete ptosis of the upper lid, and when the lid is lifted, the eye is found deviated out and down with a dilated pupil -- captioned in the lecture's own transcript as \"the classic third-nerve palsy posture\" (L5, slide 10). The oculomotor (third cranial) nerve supplies the levator palpebrae superioris (so its palsy causes ptosis), most of the extraocular muscles that adduct, elevate and depress the eye (so its palsy leaves the eye pulled out and down by the unopposed lateral rectus and superior oblique), and the parasympathetic fibres to the pupillary sphincter (so its palsy, when the pupil fibres are affected, causes a dilated, unreactive pupil -- mydriasis). All three of the stem's findings therefore point to the same single nerve.\n\nOption B, trochlear nerve, supplies only the superior oblique and its palsy produces vertical/torsional diplopia, not ptosis or mydriasis. Option C, abducent nerve, supplies only the lateral rectus, so its palsy causes an inward-deviated eye (esotropia) that cannot abduct, the opposite of the outward deviation described here, and again no ptosis or pupil involvement. Option D, facial nerve, supplies the orbicularis oculi (eyelid closure) and other facial muscles, not the levator or the extraocular muscles, so its palsy causes lagophthalmos (inability to close the eye) rather than ptosis, and has no effect on ocular deviation or pupil size.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Oculomotor nerve palsy is characterized by → Ptosis (masks the diplopia), Mydriasis (in compressive lesions e.g., aneurysm or tumour), Loss of elevation, depression, and adduction & The eye is turned outward and downward.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Ptosis with an eye deviated out and down and a dilated pupil is the classic presentation of oculomotor (third) nerve palsy, distinct from trochlear, abducent or facial nerve palsies.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1855-1856" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-5", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"Which of the following is best described as (tremulousness of the iris with eye movement)?",
  options:["Iridodialysis","Iridodonesis","Iridocyclitis","None of the above"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Iridodonesis is trembling or wobbling of the iris on eye movement, seen when the lens no longer gives the iris its normal firm posterior support -- classically in aphakia (lens absent) or a subluxated/dislocated lens where the zonules holding the lens have weakened or torn. The uvea & sclera lecture's iris overview slide (L13,14, slide 5) lists \"Tremulous iris (iridodonesis)\" as one of five headline iris signs (alongside colour change, synechiae, iris defect and rubeosis iridis), though the slide gives it as a heading only and never defines or re-explains it elsewhere in the deck. The cataract lecture's pseudophakia/aphakia comparison table (L18, slide 19) names iridodonesis directly as a \"classic sign\" of the aphakic eye, alongside a deep anterior chamber, a jet-black pupil and the absence of the lens -- all consequences of losing the lens's normal support and bulk.\n\nOption A, iridodialysis, is a different iris sign entirely: a traumatic tear of the iris root away from the ciliary body, producing a peripheral crescentic defect rather than a tremor, and it is listed under \"iris defect\" on the same overview slide as a separate branch from iridodonesis. Option C, iridocyclitis, is inflammation of the iris and ciliary body (anterior uveitis) -- a disease process with pain, redness and keratic precipitates, not a physical sign of iris movement, and it is not what the stem's description of tremulousness with eye movement refers to.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Iridodonesis means trembling of the iris during eye movement, usually due to absence of lens support (as in aphakia or subluxated lens). It indicates loss of zonular or lenticular stability.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Iridodonesis is tremulousness of the iris with eye movement, caused by loss of lens support in aphakia or lens subluxation, distinct from iridodialysis (a traumatic iris-root tear) and iridocyclitis (anterior uveitis).",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1866-1867" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-7", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"A 28-year-old female patient complains of ocular pain, excessive lacrimation together with diminished vision. Examination revealed keratic precipitates, hypopyon and ciliary injection with violaceous hue. which of the Following is an expected complication?",
  options:["Glaucoma","Cataract","Posterior synechiae","Calcific band keratopathy","All of the above"],
  answer:4,
  explanation:"The vignette's pain, lacrimation, diminished vision, keratic precipitates, hypopyon and a violaceous (ciliary) flush are the classic picture of anterior uveitis (iridocyclitis). The uvea & sclera lecture devotes two consecutive slides to exactly this question -- its \"Sequelae and complications\" slides (L13,14, slides 18-19) -- and between them list all four named complications: slide 18 gives posterior synechiae and secondary glaucoma, each illustrated with a photograph of a pupil distorted into a festooned shape by adhesions to the lens; slide 19 gives complicated cataract and band-shaped keratopathy, each with its own photograph (a dense whitish pupillary opacity for the cataract, a horizontal grey-white band across the interpalpebral cornea for the keratopathy). Because the lecture prints all four as recognised sequelae of anterior uveitis, \"all of the above\" is the correct, most complete answer.\n\nEach individual option is therefore true rather than false: posterior synechiae form when the inflamed iris adheres to the anterior lens capsule, secondary glaucoma follows from synechiae blocking aqueous outflow or from inflammatory debris clogging the trabecular meshwork, complicated cataract results from chronic inflammatory disturbance of lens metabolism, and band keratopathy follows calcium deposition in a chronically inflamed anterior segment (calcium salt deposition in Bowman's layer is described separately in the cornea lecture, L15,16). Since the question asks which is \"an expected complication\" and every option names a real one, none can be picked out as the sole exception, which is exactly why the lecture's own layout groups all four together as the disease's sequelae.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Chronic iridocyclitis may lead to posterior synechiae, secondary glaucoma, cataract formation, and band keratopathy due to prolonged inflammation.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Anterior uveitis (iridocyclitis) can lead to posterior synechiae, secondary glaucoma, complicated cataract and band-shaped keratopathy, all recognised sequelae of the disease.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1870-1871" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-12", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-vitreous",
  stem:"A 22-year-old male patient presented to the ophthalmology clinic with a complaint of seeing bugs or cobwebs together with light flashes. Ophthalmoscopy revealed an evident Weiss ring and absent red reflex. Which of the following is the best next step in the management of that patient?",
  options:["Reassurance and follow-up in 1 month","Optical coherence tomography (OCT)","CT scan of the orbit","Dilated fundus examination"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Floaters described as \"bugs or cobwebs\" together with light flashes and a Weiss ring on ophthalmoscopy are the classic presentation of acute posterior vitreous detachment (PVD): the Weiss ring is the detached, ring-shaped glial remnant that used to encircle the optic disc, now floating free in the vitreous once the posterior hyaloid has separated from the disc. The retinal detachment and degenerations lecture gives only \"aging (vitreous shrinkage)\" as the vitreous change behind detachment and never names posterior vitreous detachment or the Weiss ring (L20), so both terms are standard teaching rather than printed course material (not taken from the course material). The clinical concern with any acute, symptomatic PVD is that the separating vitreous can tug on the peripheral retina hard enough to tear it, and a retinal tear can progress to retinal detachment if missed. Because a tear can be entirely asymptomatic itself and is only found on careful peripheral examination, the correct next step for a patient presenting with acute flashes and floaters is a dilated fundus examination with scleral depression to inspect the full peripheral retina for a tear before it detaches.\n\nOption A, reassurance and follow-up in a month, is unsafe here because it defers the one examination that can catch a treatable retinal tear while it is still asymptomatic; a month's delay risks the tear progressing to a detachment. Option B, OCT, images the macula and posterior pole in cross-section and does not show the peripheral retina where PVD-related tears occur, so it is not the right tool for this specific concern. Option C, CT scan of the orbit, is not indicated at all -- there is no history of trauma or suspected orbital pathology here, and CT does not resolve retinal detail. The absent red reflex mentioned in the stem, together with the visible Weiss ring, likely reflects vitreous debris/haemorrhage obscuring the view, which is itself a reason to examine the retina promptly and thoroughly (not taken from the course material, though this reasoning follows standard clinical teaching).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Flashes, floaters, and a Weiss ring indicate posterior vitreous detachment, which can be associated with retinal tears. A dilated fundus exam is crucial to rule out retinal detachment.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Acute posterior vitreous detachment with a Weiss ring presenting with flashes and floaters requires a dilated fundus examination to exclude a retinal tear, rather than reassurance, OCT or orbital imaging.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1880-1881" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-14", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-vasc",
  stem:"Which of the following is NOT among the differential diagnosis of cherry red spot seen in ophthalmoscopy?",
  options:["CRAO / BRAO","Penetrating eye trauma","Niemann-pick disease","None of the above"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"A cherry-red spot appears when the surrounding retina turns pale and opacified from ischaemia or from lipid/ganglioside storage while the thin fovea, fed directly by the underlying choroid, stays its normal red colour by contrast -- the spot is therefore always a sign of retinal opacification around an intact fovea, never of trauma itself. The retinal vascular disorders lecture lists \"'Cherry-red spot' at macula\" among the signs of central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO) (L19.2), and the acute visual loss lecture describes a patient whose retinal examination \"reveals a white, opacified retina with a cherry-red macular spot\" in the context of sudden painless visual loss from arterial occlusion (L31,32). CRAO/BRAO is therefore a genuine cause. Storage diseases such as Niemann-Pick disease (and, more classically, Tay-Sachs disease) cause a cherry-red spot through accumulation of lipid/ganglioside material in the retinal ganglion cells surrounding the fovea, producing the same pale-retina-with-red-centre appearance (Niemann-Pick disease as a cause of the cherry-red spot is not itself printed in the ophthalmology lectures searched; this is standard teaching -- not taken from the course material).\n\nOption B, penetrating eye trauma, is the answer that does not belong: a penetrating injury damages the globe mechanically (cornea, sclera, uvea, lens, vitreous or retina directly) and does not produce the specific ischaemic/storage pallor around the fovea that generates a cherry-red spot; trauma is not part of this differential in any of the standard causes (retinal artery occlusion, storage diseases such as Tay-Sachs and Niemann-Pick, and commotio retinae (Berlin's oedema), which the trauma lecture itself illustrates with a fundus photograph labelled CHERRY-RED SPOT under BLUNT trauma (L25, slide 35/36) — blunt, never penetrating). Since CRAO/BRAO and Niemann-Pick disease are both genuine causes, and the stem asks which is NOT a cause, penetrating trauma is correctly excluded and \"none of the above\" does not apply.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A cherry-red spot is seen in central retinal artery occlusion and certain storage diseases (e.g., Tay-Sachs, Niemann-Pick), but not in penetrating trauma.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"A cherry-red spot is caused by retinal artery occlusion (CRAO/BRAO) or storage diseases such as Niemann-Pick disease, not by penetrating eye trauma.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1884-1885" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-15", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-dr",
  stem:"Which of the following is the leading cause of irreversible blindness in the western world?",
  options:["Diabetic retinopathy","Hypertensive retinopathy","AMD","CRVO","None of the above"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"The chronic visual loss lecture's own \"relevance\" slide on macular degeneration states this directly: \"In the United States, age-related macular degeneration is the leading cause of irreversible central visual loss (20/200 or worse) among people aged 52 or older\" (L35,36). Age-related macular degeneration (AMD) damages the central retina at the macula, and because it is degenerative and progressive rather than acutely treatable in its advanced (dry, geographic-atrophy) form, it accounts for more permanent, irreversible vision loss in older populations in developed countries than the other listed conditions. The same lecture separately notes glaucoma as the \"leading cause of irreversible blindness\" more broadly and as the second most important cause of blindness in the United States -- the two statements sit in the same lecture without being reconciled into a single ranking, but the AMD-specific relevance slide is the one that names the \"western world\"/United States context the stem asks about, and it is the printed source for this key.\n\nDiabetic retinopathy is a major cause of vision loss, especially in the working-age population, but it is more often at least partially treatable (panretinal photocoagulation, anti-VEGF) and is not the condition this lecture names as the leading cause in the western world. Hypertensive retinopathy is a marker of systemic vascular damage but is rarely itself the leading direct cause of blindness. CRVO (central retinal vein occlusion) can cause severe vision loss in the affected eye but is a less common, usually unilateral condition and is not ranked as a leading population-level cause of irreversible blindness in the source material.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> AMD is the leading cause of irreversible blindness in developed countries due to degeneration of the macula affecting central vision.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Age-related macular degeneration is named in the lecture as the leading cause of irreversible central visual loss in the western world, ahead of diabetic retinopathy, hypertensive retinopathy and CRVO.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1886-1887" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-18", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-dr",
  stem:"Which of the following is a cause of vision loss in diabetic retinopathy?",
  options:["Retinal artery occlusion","Optic neuritis","Vitreous hemorrhage","Retinitis pigmentosa"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"In proliferative diabetic retinopathy, chronic retinal ischaemia drives neovascularization -- new, fragile vessels growing either on the optic disc (NVD) or elsewhere on the retina (NVE), as the diabetic retinopathy lecture names them (L19.1). These abnormal vessels lack the structural integrity of normal retinal vessels and bleed easily into the vitreous cavity, producing a vitreous haemorrhage that scatters and blocks incoming light, causing sudden or progressive visual loss; the same lecture lists persistent or recurrent haemorrhage as a sign of poor involution after laser photocoagulation, and lists \"severe persistent vitreous haemorrhage\" as an indication for vitreoretinal surgery, underlining how central vitreous haemorrhage is to vision loss in this disease. Diabetic maculopathy (leakage or ischaemia at the macula) is the other major mechanism of vision loss printed in the same lecture, but among the options given here, vitreous haemorrhage is the one directly caused by the disease's proliferative vascular complications.\n\nRetinal artery occlusion is a separate vascular catastrophe (embolic or thrombotic occlusion of the central or a branch retinal artery) and, while diabetes is a risk factor for vascular disease in general, it is not itself a mechanism of vision loss within diabetic retinopathy specifically. Optic neuritis is inflammation of the optic nerve, classically linked to demyelinating disease, and is not a recognised complication of diabetic retinopathy. Retinitis pigmentosa is an inherited, progressive photoreceptor degeneration with an entirely separate pathophysiology (rod-cone dystrophy) unrelated to diabetic vascular disease (not taken from the course material for retinitis pigmentosa's definition, though its distinctness from diabetic retinopathy is standard).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In diabetic retinopathy, neovascularization can rupture, causing vitreous hemorrhage and sudden loss of vision.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Vitreous haemorrhage from fragile neovascular vessels (NVD/NVE) is a recognised cause of vision loss in proliferative diabetic retinopathy, unlike retinal artery occlusion, optic neuritis or retinitis pigmentosa.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1892-1893" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-32", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cat",
  stem:"An old male patient reports an improvement in his near vision and that he no longer needs his reading glasses, however, he reports that his far vision has deteriorated. Which of the following is the name of the described phenomenon?",
  options:["Presbyopia.","Myopic shift.","Posterior subcapsular cataract.","Second sight phenomenon."],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"As nuclear sclerosis of the lens progresses, the lens nucleus becomes denser and its refractive index rises, which increases the eye's overall refractive power and produces a myopic shift; the cataract lecture names this pairing directly and gives it its own label, \"second sight\" -- \"elderly regain near vision\" through the myopic shift, described elsewhere on the same slide set as letting the patient where \"myopic shift allows reading without glasses (temporary)\" (L18, slide 5). The lecture's own summary table for senile nuclear cataract lists \"Second sight (myopia)\" as a classic clinical feature of this specific cataract type, distinguishing it from other cataract morphologies. The phenomenon is called \"second sight\" precisely because it feels to the patient like a return of the near vision they lost to presbyopia, even though it is actually a sign of progressing cataract, not a true improvement.\n\nMyopic shift, option B, is the underlying optical mechanism (the eye becoming more myopic as the nucleus densifies) rather than the name of the overall clinical phenomenon the stem describes, which is specifically the paradoxical gain in near vision alongside worsening distance vision -- the lecture treats \"myopic shift\" and \"second sight\" as linked but distinguishable labels, with \"second sight\" being the named phenomenon asked for here. Presbyopia, option A, is the normal age-related loss of accommodation that makes reading glasses necessary in the first place, the opposite direction of change from what the stem describes, and is not caused by lens sclerosis. Posterior subcapsular cataract, option C, is a different cataract morphology that classically causes disproportionate glare and difficulty in bright light with relatively preserved distance vision in dim conditions, not the myopic near-vision improvement described here.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In nuclear sclerosis cataract, the refractive index of the lens increases, causing a myopic shift. Patients temporarily experience better near vision, known as the second sight phenomenon, before overall vision worsens.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Nuclear sclerotic cataract raises the lens's refractive index, producing a myopic shift that lets an older patient temporarily read without glasses -- the \"second sight\" phenomenon -- while distance vision worsens.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1920-1921" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-42", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A 39-year-old male presents with unilateral miosis, eyelid drooping (ptosis), and apparent enophthalmos. Which of the following is the most appropriate investigation to confirm the diagnosis?",
  options:["MRI abdomen","MRI thorax","Echocardiography","CT brain"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Miosis, ptosis and apparent enophthalmos are three of the signs the pupil and visual pathway lecture prints on its Horner syndrome slide, alongside dilation lag, anhydrosis and (in the congenital form) heterochromia (L22, slide 11) -- together these make up Horner syndrome, caused by interruption of the oculosympathetic pathway anywhere along its three-neuron chain from the hypothalamus down through the neck to the eye. The lecture separately notes that sympathetic paralysis is one of its own listed neurologic causes of an abnormal pupil (L22), but does not itself work through the localising investigations for a given lesion level; the choice of MRI thorax reflects the fact that a lesion of the first-order/second-order sympathetic chain low in the neck or apex of the chest -- classically a Pancoast (apical lung) tumour compressing the sympathetic chain -- is an important and dangerous cause of an acquired Horner syndrome in an adult, and thoracic imaging is the investigation aimed at finding it (this specific localisation and investigation choice is not printed in the searched lecture material -- not taken from the course material, though it reflects standard clinical teaching for a new adult-onset Horner syndrome).\n\nMRI abdomen is not directed at any level of the sympathetic pathway relevant to Horner syndrome and would not be expected to identify a causative lesion. Echocardiography images the heart and is unrelated to the sympathetic chain. CT brain would be relevant if a central (first-order neuron) lesion such as a brainstem or hypothalamic stroke were suspected, but the combination given here, in isolation, is the classic pattern prompting a search for a chest lesion first, making MRI thorax the best of the four listed options.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Ptosis, miosis, and enophthalmos indicate Horner’s syndrome, which can be caused by a lesion anywhere along the sympathetic chain, including apical lung tumors. MRI of the thorax helps identify a Pancoast tumor.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Miosis, ptosis and apparent enophthalmos define Horner syndrome from oculosympathetic pathway interruption; MRI thorax is used to look for a causative lesion such as an apical (Pancoast) lung tumour along the chain.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1940-1941" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-45", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-vasc",
  stem:"Which of the following is the most common cause of CRVO / BRVO?",
  options:["Embolism","Thrombosis","Arteritis","None of the above"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The retinal vascular disorders lecture's predisposing-factors slide for retinal vein occlusion lists increasing age, hypertension, diabetes and abnormalities of coagulation as systemic risk factors, and raised intraocular pressure and periphlebitis as ocular risk factors, then diagrams the pathophysiology as venous occlusion leading to stagnation, hypoxia and increased extravascular pressure, ending in oedema and haemorrhage (L19.2). The lecture's own diagram of venous occlusion does not use the word \"thrombosis\" explicitly in the searched material, but a slow-flow, stagnation-driven occlusion of a vein at a site of external compression (as at an arteriovenous crossing) is the mechanism thrombosis describes, and thrombotic occlusion at these crossing points is the standard, widely taught mechanism for both central and branch retinal vein occlusion (not taken from the course material as a verbatim statement, though it follows directly from the risk-factor and pathophysiology slides printed there).\n\nEmbolism, option A, is the mechanism for retinal artery occlusion, not vein occlusion -- an embolus lodges in and blocks an artery, and the lecture lists retinal artery occlusion (branch, cilioretinal and central) as a separate disease entity from retinal vein occlusion, each with its own distinct mechanism. Arteritis, option C, refers to inflammatory disease of arteries (such as giant cell arteritis), which is a recognised cause of retinal artery occlusion in older patients, not of venous occlusion. Since thrombosis is the correct mechanism, \"none of the above\" does not apply.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Both CRVO and BRVO most commonly result from thrombosis of the retinal vein at arteriovenous crossings. Risk factors include hypertension, diabetes, and glaucoma.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"CRVO and BRVO most commonly arise from thrombotic venous occlusion, typically at sites of arteriovenous crossing compression, distinct from the embolic mechanism of retinal artery occlusion or inflammatory arteritis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1946-1947" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-49", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-trauma",
  stem:"Which of the following has the poorest prognosis ever?",
  options:["Injury to eye by sulphuric acid","Injury to eye by hydrochloric acid","Injury to eye by sodium hydroxide","All have similar effect and prognosis"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"The trauma lecture's own comparison of alkalis and acids states that alkalis \"cause more damage as they tend to penetrate more deeply than acids and continue penetration for weeks,\" while \"acids coagulate surface proteins, forming a protective barrier against further penetration\" that limits how deep the injury goes (L25, slide 4). Sodium hydroxide (NaOH) is listed among the alkalis in the trauma lecture's chemical-injury slide (L26), together with KOH, Ca(OH)2 and NH4, and because alkalis combine with cell-membrane lipid and stromal mucopolysaccharides to achieve deep, ongoing penetration, an alkali burn such as sodium hydroxide carries the worse prognosis of the two chemical classes, consistent with progressive tissue destruction, limbal ischaemia, loss of limbal stem cells, corneal stromal opacification and later scarring, symblepharon and vascularisation described on the following slides.\n\nSulphuric acid and hydrochloric acid, options A and B, are both named acids in the same lecture, and acids coagulate surface proteins into a barrier that limits further penetration -- meaning that, while a severe acid burn can still be very damaging, the mechanism itself is self-limiting compared with an alkali's continued penetration over weeks, so an acid burn generally has a better prognosis than an equivalent alkali burn. Because sodium hydroxide is specifically an alkali and the two acids are not, option D (\"all have similar effect and prognosis\") is incorrect -- the lecture explicitly separates alkalis from acids by mechanism and severity rather than treating them as equivalent.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Alkali burns (like sodium hydroxide) penetrate deeply, causing progressive tissue destruction and scarring. They have the worst prognosis compared to acid burns.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Alkali chemical burns such as sodium hydroxide penetrate more deeply and for longer than acid burns (which form a self-limiting protein-coagulation barrier), giving alkali injuries the poorer prognosis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1954-1955" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-55", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-systemic",
  stem:"What is the grade of hypertensive retinopathy characterized by (obvious arterial narrowing with focal irregularities)?",
  options:["Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The lecture on ocular manifestations of systemic disease presents the Modified Scheie classification of hypertensive retinopathy as a four-grade scale: grade 0 is no changes, grade 1 is barely detectable narrowing, grade 2 is obvious arterial narrowing with focal irregularities, grade 3 adds retinal haemorrhages and/or exudates on top of grade 2's vascular findings, and grade 4 adds disc oedema on top of grade 3 (L23). \"Obvious arterial narrowing with focal irregularities,\" the exact phrase in the stem, is the defining feature the lecture assigns to grade 2, marking it as more advanced vascular sclerosis than the barely detectable narrowing of grade 1 but without yet showing the haemorrhages or exudates that define grade 3.\n\nGrade 1 is excluded because its narrowing is described as only \"barely detectable,\" not the \"obvious\" narrowing with focal irregularities the stem asks about. Grade 3 is excluded because it is defined as grade 2's findings plus additional retinal changes such as haemorrhages and/or exudates, which are not mentioned in the stem's description. Grade 4 is excluded for the same reason, being grade 3's findings plus optic disc swelling, a more severe and distinct additional feature not described here. The stem's wording matches grade 2 alone.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Grade 2 hypertensive retinopathy is characterized by obvious arterial narrowing with focal irregularities (arteriovenous nipping). It reflects moderate vascular sclerosis due to sustained hypertension.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"In the Modified Scheie classification, grade 2 hypertensive retinopathy is defined by obvious arterial narrowing with focal irregularities, more advanced than grade 1's barely detectable narrowing but without the haemorrhages/exudates of grade 3 or the disc oedema of grade 4.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1966-1967" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-59", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-intro-exam",
  stem:"Which of the following is the gold-standard method for IOP measurement?",
  options:["Digital method","Schiotz tonometer","Goldmann applanation tonometer","Hand held electronic tonometer"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Goldmann applanation tonometry flattens a small, fixed area of the central cornea and reads the force needed to do so; because the area flattened is fixed, the reading is largely independent of scleral rigidity, unlike the indentation methods, which is why it is treated as the reference standard against which every other method is checked. The ocular-examination lecture places it directly after the digital and indentation methods as its own dedicated slide pair, illustrating the slit-lamp-mounted instrument with its bi-prism, mobile feeder arm and adjustable control weight (L3,4, slide 38-39).\n\nOption A, the digital method, is the bedside estimate performed by asking the patient to look down and testing the globe's resistance to fluctuation with the index fingers while the other fingers rest on the forehead and temple; the same lecture's slide immediately before the applanation slides shows this technique (L3,4, slide 37), and it is a rough, examiner-dependent screening test rather than a measurement instrument. Option B, the Schiotz tonometer, works by indentation -- a plunger of known weight indents the cornea, and the amount of indentation is converted to a pressure reading via a calibration table (L3,4, slide 40); it is portable and simple but its reading is distorted by scleral rigidity, corneal thickness and even the patient's position, which is why it is not the reference method. Option D, the hand-held electronic tonometer, is a modern, portable applanation-based device that is convenient at the bedside or in a clinic without a slit lamp, but its precision is still validated against, and does not surpass, standard Goldmann applanation tonometry (not taken from the course material for this specific precision comparison, though it follows from the slide's own framing of Goldmann as the reference method).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The Goldmann applanation tonometer is the gold standard for intraocular pressure measurement, based on flattening a fixed corneal area. It provides the most accurate and reproducible readings.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Goldmann applanation tonometry, which flattens a fixed corneal area, is the gold-standard method for measuring intraocular pressure.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1974-1975" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-60", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-intro",
  stem:"Which of the following actions is performed by the superior rectus muscle?",
  options:["Elevation, adduction & intorsion.","Depression, adduction & extorsion.","Depression, abduction & intorsion.","Elevation, abduction & extorsion."],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"The anatomy and physiology lecture's own table of extraocular muscle actions lists the superior rectus with elevation as its primary action, intorsion as its secondary action and adduction as its tertiary action (L1,2, slide 69) -- so the muscle elevates the eye and, as secondary effects, also adducts and intorts it, matching option A exactly. These secondary and tertiary actions arise because the superior rectus inserts on the globe at an angle to the visual axis rather than in line with it, so its pull produces components of rotation in addition to its main elevating action (L1,2, slide 65-66, which prints the 23° angle between the visual axis and the orbital axis).\n\nOption B, depression, adduction and extorsion, is the table's row for the inferior rectus, whose primary action is depression, with extorsion secondary and adduction tertiary -- the direction of the primary action (depression instead of elevation) is what makes this wrong for the superior rectus. Option C, depression, abduction and intorsion, does not match any single muscle's row in the table -- the superior oblique has intorsion primary, but its secondary and tertiary actions are depression and abduction respectively, not paired with intorsion as its main action, so this combination misassembles two different muscles' columns. Option D, elevation, abduction and extorsion, is the row for the inferior oblique, whose primary action is extorsion (not elevation), with elevation secondary and abduction tertiary -- again the primary-action column does not match what the stem asks for the superior rectus.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The superior rectus muscle primarily elevates the eye and also contributes to adduction and intorsion. Its actions depend on the position of the eyeball relative to the visual axis.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"The superior rectus muscle's primary action is elevation, with intorsion and adduction as its secondary and tertiary actions.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1976-1977" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-65", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro-optic",
  stem:"A 68-year-old man with a history of hypertension and diabetes mellitus presents with sudden, painless loss of vision in his right eye. He denies any trauma, eye redness, or discharge. Fundoscopic examination reveals a swollen, pale optic disc with splinter hemorrhages at the disc margin. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  options:["Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (AION)","Retinal detachment","Central retinal artery occlusion","Optic neuritis"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (AION) produces sudden, painless monocular vision loss with a swollen, pale disc, and it is classically seen in older patients with vascular risk factors such as hypertension and diabetes, which is exactly this patient's profile. The optic-nerve lecture's dedicated AION slide shows this same picture directly: a swollen disc with blurred margins, a pale-yellow colour and a small flame haemorrhage at the disc margin (L21, slide 9), and the deck separately notes that the swollen disc of acute AION goes on to become pale as the nerve infarcts. Splinter/flame haemorrhages at the disc margin, as in this stem, are part of that same acute picture, reflecting ischaemia of the optic nerve head's blood supply (the short posterior ciliary arteries) rather than any retinal or vitreous process.\n\nOption B, retinal detachment, classically presents with flashes, floaters and a curtain-like visual field defect, and fundoscopy shows an elevated, folded retina rather than disc swelling -- it does not produce splinter haemorrhages at the disc margin. Option C, central retinal artery occlusion, also causes sudden painless vision loss, but the fundus shows a pale, oedematous retina with a cherry-red spot at the macula and attenuated vessels, not a swollen pale disc with margin haemorrhages -- the pathology is in the retina's blood supply, not the optic nerve head itself. Option D, optic neuritis, typically affects younger patients, is often painful (particularly on eye movement), and the disc may look normal (retrobulbar) or swollen (papillitis) but without the vascular risk-factor profile or the haemorrhagic picture described here; it is also usually associated with a relative afferent pupillary defect and colour desaturation out of proportion to acuity loss, not itself distinguishing findings emphasised in this vignette.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> AION presents with sudden, painless vision loss and a swollen pale optic disc with hemorrhages. It is due to ischemia of the optic nerve head, often linked to hypertension or diabetes.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy causes sudden painless vision loss with a swollen, pale optic disc and margin haemorrhages in patients with vascular risk factors such as hypertension and diabetes.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1986-1987" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-66", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-orbit",
  stem:"A 32-year-old woman presents with complaints of a noticeable change in her facial appearance, including swelling around her eyes and a retracted upper eyelid appearance. She also reports insomnia, palpitations, weight loss despite increased appetite, and heat intolerance over the past two months. On examination, she has exophthalmos and a fine tremor of the hands. Which investigation should be done first?",
  options:["Thyroid function tests","Orbital CT scan","Blood glucose levels","Chest X-ray"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"This patient's combination of periorbital swelling, upper-lid retraction and exophthalmos alongside systemic hyperthyroid symptoms -- palpitations, weight loss with a good appetite, heat intolerance and a fine tremor -- is the classic picture of Graves ophthalmopathy (thyroid eye disease). The orbit lecture's own slide on thyroid eye disease lists this same symptom cluster for the underlying Graves disease: weight loss despite good appetite, increased bowel frequency, sweating, heat intolerance, nervousness, palpitations, tremor and warm sweaty skin (L10, slide 15), with the ocular soft-tissue and lid-retraction signs (including the four lid signs) detailed on the following slides. Because the systemic disease drives the eye findings, confirming the thyroid status is the logical first step before ordering eye-specific imaging (not taken from the course material as an explicit \"first investigation\" statement, but it follows directly from the diagnosis the slide describes).\n\nOption B, an orbital CT scan, is useful once thyroid eye disease is suspected or confirmed, to assess extraocular muscle enlargement and orbital crowding, particularly if compressive optic neuropathy is a concern, but it does not establish the underlying thyroid diagnosis and is not the first test to order in a patient whose presentation is this classically systemic. Option C, blood glucose levels, would be relevant if a diabetic or metabolic cause were suspected, but none of the symptoms described (heat intolerance, weight loss with preserved appetite, tremor, palpitations) point toward diabetes rather than thyrotoxicosis. Option D, a chest X-ray, has no direct role in diagnosing thyroid eye disease; it might be used incidentally to screen for a substernal goitre or cardiac size in longstanding thyrotoxicosis, but it does not confirm the diagnosis suggested by this vignette.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In a patient with exophthalmos, lid retraction, and hyperthyroid symptoms, the first investigation should be thyroid function tests to confirm Graves’ disease.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"In a patient presenting with exophthalmos, lid retraction and systemic hyperthyroid symptoms suggesting Graves ophthalmopathy, thyroid function tests are the investigation to obtain first.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1988-1989" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-68", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"In iridocyclitis, which of the following is the expected change that will occur in intraocular pressure?",
  options:["It decreases","It increases","It may either decrease or increase","It is not affected"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Iridocyclitis is anterior uveitis, and the uvea lecture's own slide on its pupil and IOP signs states this variability directly: IOP is \"usually decreased but may be normal or increased\" (L13,14, slide 17). The decrease is the more common pattern, and it happens because the inflamed ciliary body produces less aqueous humour (ciliary shutdown); the increase, when it occurs, happens because inflammatory cells and protein debris clog the trabecular meshwork, or because synechiae (adhesions between the iris and lens or angle structures) obstruct aqueous outflow -- the same lecture's management slide separately notes that antiglaucoma medications are added specifically \"in case of increased IOP\" (L13,14, slide 20), which only makes sense if a rise is a recognised possibility in this disease.\n\nOption A, that IOP always decreases, ignores the fact that a rise can and does occur through the outflow-obstruction mechanisms above, so it overstates a one-directional effect. Option B, that IOP always increases, is the opposite error -- it ignores the more common ciliary-shutdown mechanism that lowers aqueous production and pressure in most affected eyes. Option D, that IOP is not affected, is directly contradicted by the lecture's own slide, which lists IOP change as one of the disease's own listed signs alongside the pupil findings -- the only question is which direction it moves in a given eye, not whether it moves at all.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In iridocyclitis (anterior uveitis), intraocular pressure (IOP) may initially decrease due to reduced aqueous humor production from ciliary body inflammation, but later increase due to blockage of the trabecular meshwork by inflammatory cells. Thus, IOP changes are variable.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"In iridocyclitis, intraocular pressure is variable — usually decreased from reduced aqueous production, but it may rise if outflow is obstructed by inflammatory debris or synechiae.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1992-1993" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-73", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cat",
  stem:"Which of the following best describes Morgagnian Cataract ?",
  options:["It's a form of mature cataract with lens cortex liquefaction causing the dark nucleus to sink inferiorly inside the lens capsule","It's a form of hypermature cataract with lens cortex liquefaction causing the dark nucleus to sink inferiorly inside the lens capsule","It's a type of cataract in which the lens nucleus acquires a dark brown to black color","None of the above"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The cataract lecture's own classification places the Morgagnian cataract as a subtype of the hypermature senile cortical cataract, defined as \"liquefaction of cortex and sinking of nucleus\" (L17, slide 13), sitting alongside the \"typical\" hypermature form (shrinkage of the capsule with calcification and cholesterol deposits). Hypermature cataracts follow the mature stage, once the lens capsule itself begins to shrink and the softened cortex liquefies into a milky fluid; in the Morgagnian variant specifically, the denser brunescent nucleus is no longer supported and settles inferiorly within the capsular bag, giving the lens a fluid-filled appearance with the nucleus visibly displaced downward.\n\nOption A describes the same cortex-liquefaction-and-sinking-nucleus mechanism but attaches it to the mature stage rather than the hypermature stage; the lecture's table places this specific finding under the hypermature heading, one stage further along than simple maturity, so labelling it \"mature\" is the wrong stage for this appearance. Option C, a lens nucleus that is dark brown to black in colour, describes a brunescent (black) cataract, which is a nuclear sclerotic cataract classified by nuclear colour rather than by cortical liquefaction and nuclear descent -- it is a real but different entity from the Morgagnian pattern being asked about. Option D, none of the above, is incorrect because option B is a faithful match to the lecture's own definition.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Morgagnian cataract is an advanced (hypermature) stage where the lens cortex liquefies, and the dense nucleus settles inferiorly. This gives a fluid-filled appearance within the capsule.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Morgagnian cataract is a hypermature senile cortical cataract in which the liquefied cortex allows the dense nucleus to sink inferiorly within the capsule.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2002-2003" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-77", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-trauma",
  stem:"A small intraocular iron foreign body:",
  options:["can be left if it doesn’t harm the eye.","must be removed.","necessitates enucleation.","can only be removed if it produces cataract"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"An intraocular iron foreign body (IOFB) must be removed regardless of its size, because retained iron dissolves and reacts with ocular tissues over time, producing progressive toxic damage to the retina, lens and other structures (siderosis bulbi) -- damage that continues even while the eye looks otherwise stable, which is why observation is not an acceptable strategy once a metallic IOFB is confirmed (L25, slide 46/47, which lists Iron (SIDEROSIS) as one of the intraocular foreign-body types, beside Copper (CHALCOSIS) and inert bodies). The trauma lecture marks \"if you suspect IOFB\" as a core topic and directs the work-up toward plain films, CT (to determine the presence, location, shape and globe integrity) and ultrasound, explicitly warning that MRI must not be done if a metallic IOFB is suspected, because the scanner's magnetic field can move the fragment and cause further damage (L25, slide 52).\n\nOption A, leaving a small IOFB if it \"doesn't harm the eye,\" is wrong because even a foreign body that appears inert on presentation carries an ongoing risk of chemical toxicity that only becomes evident later, so apparent early tolerance does not justify observation. Option C, that it necessitates enucleation, overstates the required intervention -- removal of the foreign body (often together with repair of the entry wound) is the goal, not removal of the entire eye, which is reserved for eyes that are irreparably destroyed or a source of sympathetic ophthalmia risk, not simply for the presence of a retrievable IOFB. Option D, that removal is only indicated once cataract has already developed, is backwards: waiting for a complication to develop before removing the foreign body defeats the purpose of early removal, which is precisely to prevent that and other complications from occurring.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Even a small intraocular iron foreign body must be removed because iron causes siderosis bulbi—toxic degeneration of ocular tissues. Leaving it in place risks permanent visual damage.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"A metallic intraocular foreign body must always be removed, because retained iron causes progressive toxic damage to ocular tissues regardless of the fragment's size.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2010-2011" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-79", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"Keratic precipitates are seen in:",
  options:["anterior uveitis.","posterior uveitis.","acute conjunctivitis.","trachoma"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Keratic precipitates (KPs) are clumps of inflammatory cells that settle out of the aqueous humour and deposit on the back (endothelial) surface of the cornea, and the uvea lecture lists them as one of the signs of anterior uveitis directly, on their own dedicated slide showing a slit-lamp photograph of numerous discrete white-yellow deposits scattered over the endothelium, densest inferiorly (L13,14, slide 15). They form specifically because anterior uveitis inflames the iris and ciliary body, releasing cells and protein into the anterior chamber's aqueous, which then settles by gravity onto the cooler, more posterior corneal endothelium -- a mechanism unique to anterior-segment inflammation rather than to the posterior segment or to a purely conjunctival process.\n\nOption B, posterior uveitis, inflames the choroid and retina rather than the anterior chamber, so its aqueous is not the source of the cellular debris that forms KPs; posterior uveitis is instead characterised by vitritis, chorioretinal lesions and vascular sheathing, not corneal endothelial deposits. Option C, acute conjunctivitis, is a surface infection or inflammation of the conjunctiva that produces discharge, chemosis and hyperaemia, but it does not involve the anterior chamber or its aqueous humour at all, so it cannot produce KPs. Option D, trachoma, is a chronic conjunctival and corneal surface disease caused by Chlamydia trachomatis, marked by follicles, limbal changes (Herbert's pits) and eventual scarring -- again a surface and conjunctival process rather than one that inflames the anterior chamber, so it does not produce keratic precipitates either.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Keratic precipitates (KPs) are inflammatory cell deposits on the corneal endothelium, seen in anterior uveitis (iridocyclitis). They indicate inflammation within the anterior chamber.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Keratic precipitates are inflammatory cell deposits on the corneal endothelium and are a sign of anterior uveitis (iridocyclitis).",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2014-2015" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-80", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-lac",
  stem:"Watering of the eye in a new born 2 days after birth is due to:",
  options:["conjunctivitis.","congenital naso-lacrimal duct obstruction.","trachoma.","absence of lacrimal glands"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The lacrimal system lecture's slide on congenital obstruction explains that the lower end of the nasolacrimal duct, at the valve of Hasner, is the last part of the drainage system to canalise, and that complete patency most commonly occurs only soon after birth -- so a newborn examined within the first days of life very plausibly still has an imperforate valve of Hasner, producing epiphora (watering) from birth or the first days of life (L9, slide 21). The same slide notes that epiphora from this cause affects at least 20% of neonates, with spontaneous resolution in about 85% within the first year, and that matting of the lashes and mucopurulent reflux on pressure over the sac are also part of the picture; acute dacryocystitis, by contrast, is stated to be very rare at this age.\n\nOption A, conjunctivitis, produces redness, discharge and irritation from an infective or inflammatory surface process, but it is not the expected explanation for isolated watering appearing this early and this specifically at day 2 of life, and the lecture's own differential-diagnosis line for congenital watering lists chronic conjunctivitis only as one of several alternative causes to rule out, not as the leading explanation. Option C, trachoma, is a chronic conjunctival infection from repeated or prolonged exposure to Chlamydia trachomatis; it takes time to develop and would not present as isolated watering in a 2-day-old infant. Option D, absence of the lacrimal glands, would in fact cause a dry eye from too little tear production rather than watering, the opposite clinical picture from what the stem describes, and it is not the mechanism behind neonatal epiphora at all.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Watering of the eye in a newborn within the first days of life is most commonly due to congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction at the still-imperforate valve of Hasner.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2016" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-81", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cat",
  stem:"LASER can be used to treat the following conditions EXCEPT:",
  options:["neovascular glaucoma.","rubbing lashes.","dendritic ulcer.","cataract"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Cataract is the exception because a cataract -- an opacified lens -- is removed surgically (by phacoemulsification or extracapsular extraction), not treated away by a laser; the cataract lecture describes femtosecond laser-assisted cataract surgery (FLACS), in which a laser creates the corneal incision, the capsulorhexis and fragments the nucleus, but this is an assistive step within a surgical procedure -- the opacified lens material is still physically removed by the surgeon, not dissolved or corrected by the laser alone (L18, slide 15). The one situation in which a laser genuinely treats a lens-related problem on its own is posterior capsule opacification (PCO) after cataract surgery, which is cleared with an Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy (L18, slide 18) -- but that treats a secondary membrane left behind after surgery, not the cataract itself.\n\nOption A, neovascular glaucoma, is a form of secondary angle-closure glaucoma in which a fibrovascular membrane grows over the trabecular meshwork and contracts, pulling the iris over the angle (L12, slide 26, the secondary narrow-angle glaucoma slide, where it is the first listed example); its underlying retinal ischaemia is treated with panretinal laser photocoagulation to regress the new vessels, and laser iridotomy or trabeculoplasty techniques are also part of the glaucoma treatment ladder more broadly (L19.2, slide 14, which tells you to look for rubeosis and angle new vessels after an ischaemic occlusion and to treat neovascularization by panretinal photocoagulation; laser trabeculoplasty is L11, slide 19, and laser iridotomy L12, slides 16 and 22). Option B, rubbing lashes (trichiasis), can be treated with laser epilation/ablation of the misdirected lash follicles to stop them rubbing against the cornea (not taken from the course material). Option C, dendritic ulcer (herpetic keratitis), is counted by the printed key among the laser-treatable conditions; the cornea lecture prints only \"Debridement of infected epithelium (swab) can be adjunctive\" (L15,16, slide 45), with no laser, and the recognised laser role in herpetic corneal disease is excimer phototherapeutic keratectomy for residual superficial scarring rather than the active ulcer (not taken from the course material) -- in all three of these, unlike cataract, the laser can act directly on the diseased tissue itself rather than only assisting a surgical removal.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Laser is used directly to treat conditions such as neovascular glaucoma, trichiasis and dendritic ulcer, but a cataract itself is removed surgically rather than treated by laser alone.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2017-2018" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-2", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-conj",
  stem:"Symblepharon may be caused by:",
  options:["chemical burn.","spring catarrh.","phlycten.","epidemic keratoconjunctivitis"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Symblepharon is the adhesion of the palpebral to the bulbar conjunctiva, and the lecture on chemical injuries places it explicitly in the late/chronic phase (>7 days) of a severe chemical burn, alongside dry eye and entropion: \"Conjunctiva: scarring. Dry eye, symblepharon, entropion\" (L26, page 9). Alkalis in particular penetrate deeply and destroy the conjunctival goblet cells and stroma, and the healing fibrosis that follows bridges the fornix with scar bands — exactly the appearance the deck's own conjunctiva lecture shows on its symblepharon photograph, a lower fornix obliterated by pale scarred bands (L6, slide 63). Spring catarrh (vernal keratoconjunctivitis) is a chronic allergic, itchy conjunctivitis with papillary hypertrophy and Trantas dots; it inflames the conjunctiva without the full-thickness necrosis needed to fuse the two conjunctival layers, and the lecture material never links it to symblepharon (not taken from the course material). Phlyctenular conjunctivitis produces small, self-limited hypersensitivity nodules at the limbus that heal without the deep cicatrizing damage a chemical burn causes, so it is not a recognised cause of symblepharon either (not taken from the course material). Epidemic keratoconjunctivitis is an acute adenoviral infection that, even when severe enough to leave subepithelial infiltrates or pseudomembranes, resolves without the tissue-destructive scarring seen after alkali or acid exposure, and no source material connects it to symblepharon (not taken from the course material). Chemical burn is therefore the only option among the four whose pathophysiology and lecture citation both support fusion of the conjunctival surfaces.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Symblepharon is adhesion between the bulbar and palpebral conjunctiva, commonly caused by chemical burns or severe conjunctival inflammation. The scarring process leads to fusion of the conjunctival surfaces.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify chemical burn as a cause of symblepharon (conjunctival scarring adhesion)",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2026-2027" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-3", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-refract-astig",
  stem:"Astigmatism is NOT corrected by:",
  options:["glasses.","contact lenses.","radial keratotomy.","LASIK"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"The lecture's treatment tree for astigmatism lists only glasses (cylindrical lenses), soft toric contact lenses, LASIK, PRK, lenticular surgery, rigid gas-permeable lenses, intracorneal ring segments and keratoplasty as the recognised options for regular and irregular astigmatism (L7,8, slide 71). Radial keratotomy is never named anywhere in that tree or anywhere else in the deck, which is consistent with its real role: it is a set of deep radial corneal incisions historically used to flatten the cornea and reduce myopia, and it does not reshape the cornea's meridional curvature difference that defines astigmatism (not taken from the course material). Glasses correct astigmatism with cylindrical lenses that neutralise the eye's two focal lines, exactly as the deck's treatment tree states under \"Conservative → Glasses (Cylindrical lenses)\" (L7,8, slide 71), so option A is a valid correction and not the answer. Contact lenses correct astigmatism through soft toric lenses for regular astigmatism or rigid gas-permeable lenses for irregular astigmatism, both listed on the same slide (L7,8, slide 71), so option B is also a valid correction. LASIK is listed directly under \"Corneal surgery\" in the regular-astigmatism surgical branch of the same tree (L7,8, slide 71), confirming it is an accepted surgical correction, so option D is valid as well. Because radial keratotomy targets spherical myopic power rather than astigmatic curvature and is absent from the lecture's own treatment options, it is the one procedure among the four that does NOT correct astigmatism, matching option C.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Astigmatism is corrected with glasses, contact lenses, or LASIK, but not with radial keratotomy, which is designed mainly for myopia. It involves radial corneal incisions that don’t correct irregular curvature.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify radial keratotomy as a procedure that does not correct astigmatism",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2028-2029" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-4", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-lid",
  stem:"Ptosis cannot be caused by:",
  options:["third nerve palsy.","Horner’s syndrome.","large chalazion of the lower lid.","trauma"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Ptosis is drooping of the UPPER eyelid, and a large chalazion of the LOWER lid cannot produce it because the lower lid is anatomically and mechanically independent of the levator/upper-lid apparatus that ptosis affects — a lower-lid mass has no attachment to the levator complex and cannot pull the upper lid down (not taken from the course material, though it follows directly from the anatomical separation of the two lids). By contrast, the lecture's own ptosis slides map the recognised causes directly onto upper-lid pathology: slide 10 shows complete ptosis from \"Oculomotor paralysis\" (third nerve palsy), demonstrating a neurogenic cause (L5, slide 10); slide 11 shows partial ptosis with a narrowed palpebral fissure captioned \"Sympathetic paralysis (Horner Syndrome)\" (L5, slide 11), confirming Horner's syndrome as a recognised cause; and slide 14, headed \"Ptosis / Mechanical,\" shows an infant with a large upper-lid vascular mass mechanically dragging the lid down and occluding the eye, establishing that a mass involving the UPPER lid is a valid mechanical cause of ptosis (L5, slide 14). Trauma is not named explicitly as a ptosis cause in this deck, but traumatic disruption or dehiscence of the levator aponeurosis producing acquired ptosis is standard mechanism consistent with the mechanical/aponeurotic causes the slides do illustrate (not taken from the course material). Because options A, B and D are all upper-lid or neurogenic mechanisms the lecture supports (or that are anatomically plausible upper-lid causes), and a lower-lid chalazion cannot mechanically affect the upper lid, option C is correctly the one condition that cannot cause ptosis.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Ptosis is drooping of the upper eyelid caused by conditions like third nerve palsy, Horner’s syndrome, or trauma. A chalazion of the lower lid doesn’t affect the upper lid’s position.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise that a lower-lid chalazion cannot cause ptosis because ptosis is an upper-lid sign",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2030-2031" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-5", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-vasc",
  stem:"Fluorescein angiography is used for studying:",
  options:["orbital vessels.","retinal vessels.","lid vessels.","conjunctival vessels"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Fluorescein angiography (FA) is designed to visualise the retinal and choroidal circulation. The lecture shows a classic example: “FA of branch retinal vein occlusion – Early – blocked background fluorescence due to haemorrhage; Late – hyperfluorescence due to diffuse oedema” (L19.2, page 6). A similar description appears for arterial lesions: “FA of branch retinal artery occlusion – Early masking; Extreme delay of arterial phase; Late staining of arterial walls” (L19.2, page 24). These passages demonstrate that the technique highlights retinal vessels and their perfusion abnormalities, confirming that option B is correct. Option A (orbital vessels) is incorrect because the lecture never mentions FA being applied to the orbit; orbital vascular imaging is typically performed with MRI or CT angiography, not fluorescein (not taken from the course material). Option C (lid vessels) is also wrong; the lid vasculature is superficial and examined clinically or with Doppler, and FA is not used for that purpose (not taken from the course material). Finally, option D (conjunctival vessels) is false; while conjunctival angiography can be performed with other dyes, fluorescein angiography is not employed for conjunctival circulation in the material presented (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Fluorescein angiography highlights retinal and choroidal circulation, allowing assessment of vascular diseases like diabetic retinopathy or vein occlusion. It does not visualize orbital or conjunctival vessels.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify which ocular vascular structures are visualised by fluorescein angiography",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2032-2033" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-6", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"Pars planitis is:",
  options:["anterior uveitis.","intermediate uveitis.","posterior uveitis.","panuveitis."],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The lecture's anatomical classification of uveitis defines intermediate uveitis as \"Inflammation predominantly involving the pars plana, vitreous, and the peripheral retina\" (L13,14, slide 12). Pars planitis is the idiopathic subtype of intermediate uveitis in which this pars-plana-centred inflammation occurs without an identifiable systemic cause; the term \"pars planitis\" itself is not used in the lecture, so the specific name-to-category link is (not taken from the course material), but it is a direct and standard consequence of the anatomical definition the slide gives — inflammation of the pars plana is, by the lecture's own classification, intermediate uveitis. Anterior uveitis, the first category on the same slide, is defined as iritis/iridocyclitis — inflammation of the iris and ciliary body at the front of the eye, not the pars plana — so it does not match pars planitis (L13,14, slide 12). Posterior uveitis is defined as choroiditis or chorioretinitis, inflammation of the choroid with or without the retina, which is a different, more posterior compartment than the pars plana (L13,14, slide 12). Panuveitis is defined as simultaneous inflammation of all three parts of the uveal tract together, which is broader than the isolated pars-plana process that defines pars planitis (L13,14, slide 12). Because the pars plana sits within the middle segment the lecture assigns to the intermediate category, and none of the other three categories are anatomically centred there, intermediate uveitis is the only correct classification.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Classify pars planitis as intermediate uveitis based on its pars-plana location",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2034-2035" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-7", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-pupil",
  stem:"Inferior oblique muscle is the only:",
  options:["elevator of the abducted eye.","elevator of the adducted eye.","depressor of the abducted eye.","depressor of the adducted eye"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The lecture's high-yield action table gives the inferior oblique's action as \"elevation (Mainly in adduction)\" (L3,4, slide 43), and the six-cardinal-positions diagram on the following slide confirms this by naming the inferior oblique as the muscle tested for the up-and-in gaze position (L3,4, slide 44). Because elevation of the globe requires the eye to be adducted for the inferior oblique to act as the prime elevator, and no other muscle produces elevation specifically in the adducted position, it is correctly described as the only elevator of the adducted eye. The superior rectus is also an elevator, but the same table lists its action as \"elevation, (Mainly in abduction)\" (L3,4, slide 43) — it elevates best when the eye is abducted, the opposite position, so option A (elevator of the abducted eye) describes the superior rectus, not the inferior oblique. The inferior rectus is a depressor rather than an elevator, with its action given as \"depression (Mainly in abduction)\" on the same table (L3,4, slide 43), so it cannot be the answer to either elevator option and rules out any depressor-of-the-abducted-eye reading (option C) being assigned to the inferior oblique. The superior oblique is the muscle that depresses the adducted eye, with its action listed as \"depression (Mainly in adduction)\" (L3,4, slide 43), which is exactly option D — the mirror-image depressor role to the inferior oblique's elevator role — confirming that option D belongs to the superior oblique and not the inferior oblique. This leaves elevation of the adducted eye, option B, as the inferior oblique's unique action among the four choices.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The inferior oblique muscle elevates the eye when it is adducted. It also contributes to extorsion and abduction.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the inferior oblique as the sole elevator of the adducted eye",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2036-2037" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-10", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cornea",
  stem:"Topical corticosteroids are contraindicated in the following type of corneal ulcers:",
  options:["catarrhal ulcer.","shield ulcer.","dendritic ulcer.","fascicular ulcer."],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"The dendritic ulcer is the classic epithelial presentation of herpes simplex keratitis, and the lecture states this contraindication as a named clinical pearl: \"NEVER use topical steroids in EPITHELIAL herpes keratitis – this promotes viral replication and can result in corneal perforation\" (L15,16, page 16), with the epithelial type itself defined as \"Active virus replication in epithelium\" and a \"Classic dendritic ulcer with terminal buds\" (L15,16, page 14). Because active viral replication in the epithelium is the driving process in a dendritic ulcer, suppressing the local immune response with a steroid removes the check on that replication and risks progression to a geographic ulcer or perforation, exactly as the lecture's worked scenario states: \"Correct Answer: Topical antiviral... NO TOPICAL STEROIDS\" for a dendritic ulcer case (L15,16, page 45). A catarrhal (marginal) ulcer is an immune-mediated hypersensitivity reaction to staphylococcal antigens, and the lecture's treatment for it explicitly includes topical steroids alongside blepharitis treatment: \"treat blepharitis + topical steroids\" (L15,16, page 35), so steroids are not contraindicated there. A shield ulcer is not covered in this cornea lecture; it is a sterile epithelial defect from mechanical rubbing of giant papillae in vernal keratoconjunctivitis, and topical steroids are part of its standard management to control the underlying allergic inflammation (not taken from the course material). A fascicular ulcer is a peripheral corneal ulcer that migrates centrally, most often bacterial or associated with vascularisation, and it is not linked anywhere in the lecture to a steroid contraindication, since the deck's steroid warnings are specifically tied to active herpetic epithelial replication (not taken from the course material). Only the dendritic (herpetic epithelial) ulcer carries the lecture's explicit steroid contraindication.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Corticosteroids worsen herpes simplex keratitis (dendritic ulcer) by promoting viral replication and corneal perforation. Antiviral therapy should be used instead.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the dendritic (herpetic epithelial) corneal ulcer as the one where topical steroids are contraindicated",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2042-2043" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-11", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-trauma",
  stem:"Post-traumatic diplopia may be due to:",
  options:["iridodonesis.","iridodialysis.","angle recession glaucoma.","uniocular hyphema."],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Iridodialysis, the separation of the iris root from the ciliary body, is the only option that directly produces monocular diplopia after trauma. The lecture marks this relationship on slide 29, stating “Iridodialysis — (UNIOCULAR DIPLOPIA)” (L25, slide 29). When the iris is torn away, an abnormal pupil edge creates a second pseudo‑pupil that splits the visual axis, giving the patient double vision in the affected eye. Iridodonesis, listed in option A, describes a trembling iris that oscillates with eye movements and is usually seen with lens subluxation; the lecture prints \"Tremulous iris (iridodonesis)\" as a bare heading (L13,14, slide 5) and L18 lists it among the classic signs of aphakia, but it does not generate an extra visual axis and therefore does not cause diplopia (not taken from the course material). Angle‑recession glaucoma, option C, is a late sequela of blunt trauma that raises intra‑ocular pressure and may produce visual field loss, but it is not associated with double vision (not taken from the course material). Uniocular hyphema, option D, is blood accumulating in the anterior chamber; the slides on hyphema (slides 25‑28) cover grading, re-bleeding, raised IOP and corneal blood staining, but never link it to diplopia (L25, slides 25‑28). Consequently, B is the only choice that matches the lecture content and the pathophysiology of post‑traumatic diplopia.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Iridodialysis is separation of the iris root from the ciliary body following trauma, which can cause monocular diplopia. It results in an additional pupil-like opening.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify which post‑traumatic ocular condition can cause diplopia",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2044-2045" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-12", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A right optic tract lesion results in:",
  options:["right homonymous hemianopia.","left homonymous hemianopia.","bitemporal hemianopia.","binasal hemianopia."],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The lecture's own summary lesion map works through exactly this scenario: for \"lesion C (right optic tract)\" it states \"Left field: LEFT half black, right half grey. Right field: LEFT half black, right half grey. = LEFT HOMONYMOUS HEMIANOPIA (same side lost in both eyes)\" (L22, slide 24). This matches the dedicated optic tract slide earlier in the deck, which prints the caption \"Contralateral homonymous hemianopia (incongruous)\" directly under the tract lesion figure (L22, slide 17). The optic tract carries crossed fibres from the contralateral nasal retina and uncrossed fibres from the ipsilateral temporal retina — together these represent the visual field on the side opposite the tract, so a right-sided lesion knocks out the left half of each eye's field, producing LEFT, not right, homonymous hemianopia; this rules out option A. Bitemporal hemianopia is the map's lesion B, at the midline of the chiasm, where only the crossing nasal fibres from both eyes are cut, sparing the temporal fields — the lecture's own row for that lesion reads \"Right field: RIGHT half black, left half grey\" for the right eye (the left field of the same row reads \"LEFT half black\"), a chiasmal pattern quite different from a post-chiasmal tract lesion (L22, slide 24), so option C does not fit a tract lesion. Binasal hemianopia is not produced by any single lesion on the lecture's five-point map (A–E all lie on the retina-to-cortex pathway, none isolating the temporal fibres from both sides), and it is not associated with the optic tract in the source material (not taken from the course material), so option D is excluded. The lecture's own worked example therefore directly supports left homonymous hemianopia as the answer.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A right optic tract lesion affects fibers from the left visual fields of both eyes, producing left homonymous hemianopia. This occurs after the optic chiasm.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Localise a right optic tract lesion to a left homonymous hemianopia",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2046-2047" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-13", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-squint",
  stem:"The most conclusive test for diagnosing esotropia is:",
  options:["corneal light reflex test.","cover test.","pin-hole test.","Worth's 4 dot test"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The lecture's own cover-test slide is built around exactly this diagnosis, showing a child photograph where \"the eye on the RIGHT of the photograph is TURNED IN toward the nose... = a manifest convergent deviation — ESOTROPIA of the right-of-photograph eye\" (L29, slide 11), naming the cover test as the tool used to demonstrate the manifest deviation. The cover test is the most conclusive method because it works by covering the fixing eye and objectively watching the deviating eye take up fixation (or covering one eye and watching for movement on uncovering) — a direct, observable movement-based sign of misalignment, rather than an inference from a reflected light position. The corneal light reflex test (Hirschberg) is only an estimate: it infers the angle of deviation from where a light reflex falls on the cornea, and the lecture's own critique notes the deck shows the reflex displaced on its photographs while the deck's own note on the slide records that the cover test itself is not performed in this photograph (L29, slide 11), making it a rougher, indirect method compared with the movement seen on covering an eye. The pin-hole test is a visual acuity test used to determine whether reduced vision is due to a refractive error, and it has no role in detecting or measuring an ocular deviation such as esotropia (not taken from the course material). Worth's four-dot test assesses binocular single vision and fusion status (or suppression/diplopia) using red-green glasses and a four-dot target; it evaluates the sensory consequences of a squint rather than directly and conclusively demonstrating the motor deviation itself, so it is less definitive for diagnosing esotropia than the cover test (not taken from the course material). The cover test remains the most direct, conclusive clinical sign of a manifest squint like esotropia.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The cover test objectively detects latent or manifest squints (like esotropia) by observing eye movement when the fellow eye is covered. It is more reliable than the corneal reflex test.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the cover test as the most conclusive clinical test for diagnosing esotropia",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2048-2049" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-14", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-lac",
  stem:"A 70-year old woman complains of chronic tearing and discharge. Irrigation of the lower canaliculus produces a mucopurulent regurgitation from the upper canaliculus after distending the lacrimal sac. Which of the following statements is true?",
  options:["Jones one testing will reveal dye in the nose","there is probably a common canalicular obstruction","this condition is usually cured permanently by antibiotics","DCR is the treatment of choice"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"The lecture defines the regurge test used in this exact scenario: \"Mucopurulent discharge on compression is indicative of a patent canalicular system, but with an obstruction distal to the lower end of the lacrimal sac\" (L9, slide 12). Irrigating the lower canaliculus and distending the sac, then seeing mucopurulent material reflux out of the UPPER canaliculus, therefore proves the canaliculi themselves are open and the block sits at or beyond the lacrimal sac — i.e. in the nasolacrimal duct — which in a 70-year-old with chronic tearing and discharge is the picture of chronic dacryocystitis from nasolacrimal duct obstruction. The lecture lists DCR (dacryocystorhinostomy) as the surgical treatment for acquired nasolacrimal obstruction, naming \"External DCR\" and \"Endoscopic DCR\" as the treatment options (L9, slide 29), making DCR the correct definitive treatment and matching option D. Jones I dye testing checks whether fluorescein instilled in the conjunctival sac reaches the nose through a patent system, with \"Positive recovery in the nose = patent lacrimal system\" and \"Negative recovery... = lower duct obstruction\" (L9, slide 18); since this patient has a duct-level block, Jones I would be expected to be NEGATIVE, not show dye in the nose, so option A is wrong. The reflux out of the upper canaliculus itself demonstrates that the canalicular system is patent, which the lecture's regurge-test definition directly contradicts a common canalicular obstruction (L9, slide 12) — a true canalicular block would prevent, not produce, this reflux pattern — so option B is wrong. Antibiotics can treat superimposed infection but cannot permanently cure a structural, distal outflow obstruction, which needs surgical bypass; the lecture does not present antibiotics as curative for this mechanical blockage, so option C is wrong.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The mucopurulent regurgitation from the upper canaliculus after irrigation and distension of the sac indicates an obstruction in the nasolacrimal drainage system, typically at or beyond the lacrimal sac.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise that upper-canaliculus reflux after lower-canaliculus irrigation indicates nasolacrimal duct obstruction requiring DCR",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2050-2051" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-15", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"Horner's syndrome is characterized by all of the following except:",
  options:["miosis","widened palpebral fissure","slight enophthalmos","unilateral absence of sweating"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The lecture's Horner Syndrome slide lists its features exactly: \"Miosis, Dilation Lag, Ptosis, Apparent enophthalmos, Anhydrosis, Heterochromia (Congenital)\" (L22, slide 11), and its clinical photograph shows the affected eye with \"the palpebral fissure of the left-of-photograph eye... slightly narrower than its fellow\" because of the accompanying ptosis (L22, slide 11). Because ptosis of the upper lid on the affected side NARROWS the palpebral fissure, a WIDENED palpebral fissure is the opposite of what Horner's syndrome produces, making it the one feature not part of the syndrome and the correct exception. Miosis is explicitly the first sign listed on the slide (L22, slide 11) — the affected pupil is smaller due to loss of sympathetic dilator tone — so it is a true feature of Horner's syndrome, not the exception. Slight enophthalmos is also explicitly listed as \"Apparent enophthalmos\" (L22, slide 11); the eye appears sunken because of the mild ptosis and narrowed fissure even though true globe position is usually unchanged, so this too is a genuine feature and not the exception. Unilateral absence of sweating is listed on the same slide as \"Anhydrosis\" (L22, slide 11), reflecting loss of sympathetic innervation to facial sweat glands on the affected side, so it is also a true feature. Since miosis, enophthalmos and anhidrosis are all directly listed on the lecture's Horner slide and ptosis narrows rather than widens the fissure, \"widened palpebral fissure\" is correctly identified as the feature that does NOT belong to Horner's syndrome.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Horner’s syndrome presents with ptosis, miosis, enophthalmos, and anhidrosis. The palpebral fissure is narrowed, not widened.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise that Horner's syndrome narrows, rather than widens, the palpebral fissure",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2052-2053" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-33", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-dr",
  stem:"65 years old patient complaining of gradual decrease visual acuity in both eyes in the last 3 months. Medical history of diabetes for more than 20 years. Visual acuity is 6/24 in both eyes. Patient is pseudophakic in both eyes with clear media. Fundus examination revealed bilateral diabetic macular edema and mild NPDR.\n\nWhat further tests could help in diagnosis?",
  options:["Visual field test","OCT for optic nerve","Amsler grid test","Fluoresceine angiography"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Fluorescein angiography is the investigation of choice because it directly demonstrates the presence of retinal micro‑aneurysms and the pattern of macular leakage that defines diabetic maculopathy. The lecture notes that focal diabetic maculopathy shows \"Focal leakage on FA\" (L19.1, page 12) and that diffuse maculopathy is characterised by \"Generalized leakage on FA\" (L19.1, page 13). Ischaemic maculopathy is identified by \"Capillary non‑perfusion on FA\" (L19.1, page 14). These statements confirm that fluorescein angiography visualises the lesions that guide laser or anti‑VEGF therapy, exactly as the printed key indicates.\n\nA visual field test is designed to map peripheral field defects, not macular fluid or leakage, and the lecture contains no reference to its use in diabetic macular edema (not taken from the course material). OCT of the optic nerve evaluates nerve‑head thickness for glaucoma or optic neuropathy, which is unrelated to macular oedema; the lecture does not discuss OCT in this context (not taken from the course material). The Amsler grid can detect metamorphopsia but does not quantify leakage or guide treatment; the course material does not mention it for diabetic maculopathy (not taken from the course material). Therefore the only answer that aligns with the lecture content is option D.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This test visualizes retinal microaneurysms and leakage at the macula. It helps guide laser or anti-VEGF treatment.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the diagnostic test that visualises macular leakage in diabetic macular edema.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2088-2089" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-34", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-dr",
  stem:"65 years old patient complaining of gradual decrease visual acuity in both eyes in the last 3 months. Medical history of diabetes for more than 20 years. Visual acuity is 6/24 in both eyes. Patient is pseudophakic in both eyes with clear media. Fundus examination revealed bilateral diabetic macular edema and mild NPDR.\n\nWhat is the appropriate treatment for this patient?",
  options:["YAG laser capsulotomy","PRP laser","Intravitreal Anti-VEGF","Oral antioxidant supplements"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"The correct answer is C because the patient has clinically significant diabetic macular oedema (DME) with visual loss. The lecture states that for diffuse retinal thickening, \"Intra vitreal Injection of anti VEGEF\" is the recommended treatment (L19.1, page 15). Anti‑VEGF agents reduce vascular permeability and resolve macular swelling, thereby improving visual acuity, which matches the clinical scenario. Option A, YAG laser capsulotomy, is used to treat posterior capsule opacification after cataract surgery; the case notes clear media and no mention of capsular opacity, so it is not indicated (not taken from the course material). Option B, pan‑retinal photocoagulation (PRP), is reserved for proliferative diabetic retinopathy or severe neovascularization, as the lecture lists PRP under \"Indications for treatment of proliferative diabetic retinopathy\" (L19.1, page 16) and not for macular oedema. The patient only has mild NPDR and macular edema, so PRP would be unnecessary and could worsen macular function. Option D, oral antioxidant supplements, is not discussed in the lecture as a therapy for DME and lacks evidence for efficacy in this condition (not taken from the course material). Therefore, intravitreal anti‑VEGF therapy is the only appropriate choice.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Anti-VEGF injections reduce vascular permeability and macular swelling.\nThey are first-line therapy for diabetic macular edema.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the first‑line treatment for clinically significant diabetic macular oedema.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2090-2091" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-44", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cornea-surg",
  stem:"Seeing colored halos of gradual onset and slowly progressive course in a young male with frequent changing glasses prescription is most probably:",
  options:["Acute glaucoma","Senile cataract","Uveitis","Keratoconus"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"The lecture describes keratoconus as a \"progressive bilateral (often asymmetric) corneal thinning and protrusion\" with an \"Onset: puberty; progression until 3rd-4th decade,\" fitting a young patient with a slowly progressive course (L15,16, page 23). Because the ectatic, irregularly steepening cornea continuously changes its astigmatic power, patients need their spectacle prescription changed repeatedly as the irregular astigmatism worsens — a pattern the lecture supports indirectly through its early management step of \"Spectacles (early, mild)\" being superseded as the disease advances (L15,16, page 25) — while the specific symptom description \"coloured halos\" is not printed in this lecture and is added from general knowledge of the glare and monocular ghosting produced by irregular astigmatism (not taken from the course material). Acute glaucoma classically causes SUDDEN, severe pain with red eye and corneal oedema producing haloes, not a gradual, slowly progressive course over years in an otherwise comfortable young patient, so its acute presentation does not match this vignette. Senile cataract is a disease of older age with gradually clouding, not changing refractive astigmatism, and does not typically drive the repeated changes in spectacle prescription described, nor does the lecture connect cataract to a young male's frequent refraction changes (not taken from the course material, but excluded by age and mechanism). Uveitis presents with pain, redness, photophobia and often blurred vision from inflammatory changes rather than a slow refractive drift requiring repeated new glasses, and the lecture's uveitis material does not describe halos or progressive refractive change as a hallmark (not taken from the course material). Keratoconus, with its puberty-onset, gradually progressive corneal irregularity and consequent search for optical correction, is the diagnosis the lecture's own natural history most directly supports.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Gradual onset of colored halos with frequent changes in glasses in a young person suggests irregular astigmatism due to keratoconus. It’s a progressive corneal ectatic disorder.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise keratoconus from gradual visual disturbance and frequent spectacle changes in a young patient",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2110-2111" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-45", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-sclera",
  stem:"A painful , tender , non-itchy localized redness of conjunctiva can be caused by which of the folloeing pathology ?",
  options:["Bulbar spring catarrh","Episcleritis","Vascular pterygium","Phlyctenular conjunctivitis"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The lecture's episcleritis slide describes exactly this presentation: \"Relatively common, benign and self-limited cause of red eye... Presented with: Red eye, Mild pain and/or discomfort, Mild localised ocular tenderness, No discharge (if present: minimal & watery)\" (L13,14, slide 30), which matches a painful, tender, localised, non-discharging redness. The lecture also notes episcleritis is usually unilateral, consistent with a single localized area of redness rather than a diffuse allergic process (L13,14, slide 30). Bulbar spring catarrh (vernal keratoconjunctivitis affecting the bulbar conjunctiva) is fundamentally an ITCHY allergic condition with papillary or limbal changes, not a tender, non-itchy localized redness, so it does not match this stem's key negative feature of being non-itchy (not taken from the course material, but excluded by its defining itch). Vascular pterygium is a wing-shaped fibrovascular growth encroaching on the cornea from the conjunctiva; it may become inflamed and red but presents as a visible growth rather than a localized, tender patch of redness without a mass lesion, so it does not fit this description either (not taken from the course material). Phlyctenular conjunctivitis produces a small nodular hypersensitivity lesion at the limbus that is typically associated with photophobia, tearing and foreign-body sensation from an accompanying corneal component, rather than the mild, purely localized tenderness the lecture assigns to episcleritis, and the lecture does not describe it as tender and non-itchy in this way (not taken from the course material). Episcleritis, with its lecture-defined combination of localized redness, mild tenderness, absence of itching, and absence of discharge, is the best match for the stem.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Localized, tender, non-itchy redness without discharge points to episcleritis, a benign inflammation of the episcleral tissue. It’s self-limiting and less painful than scleritis.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise episcleritis from painful, tender, non-itchy localized conjunctival redness",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2112-2113" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-46", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-trauma",
  stem:"6 years old child presented with white pupil of his right eye that appeared for the first time since 2 months following trauma to the right eye with stick. The parents assure that the vision in the affected eye was completely normal in previous examinations prior to the trauma. Which of the following pathologies is the possible cause of leukocoria in this patient?",
  options:["Cataract","Retinoblastoma","Retinopathy of prematurity","Corneal degeneration"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Cataract is the keyed answer because blunt trauma is a recognised direct cause of an acute lens opacity, and the lecture names this exact picture: “SLIDE 32/33 — Blunt trauma: Rosette-shaped cataract” with a red-reflex photograph illustrating it (L25, slide 32/33). A stick injury to the globe is blunt trauma, and a rosette-shaped (posterior subcapsular, star-shaped) cataract forming over the following weeks to months after such an injury is a well-described post-traumatic lens change, which fits a white pupil appearing two months after the stick injury in a child whose vision was normal beforehand.\n\nRetinoblastoma, option B, is excluded by the history rather than by absence of leukocoria as a feature: the lecture on leukocoria differentials lists retinoblastoma as the \"most dangerous\" cause of a white pupil in a child (L33,34, page 18), but retinoblastoma is an intraocular tumour that is already present and growing before any trauma, so it would not explain a previously and repeatedly documented normal eye that only turns white after a discrete traumatic event two months earlier. The temporal link to trauma with a prior normal record argues against a pre-existing tumour and for an acquired traumatic cataract instead.\n\nRetinopathy of prematurity, option C, is a disease of preterm, low-birth-weight infants with abnormal retinal vascularisation, and the lecture's own leukocoria list ties it to prematurity, not to an otherwise normal 6-year-old with a clear traumatic precipitant (L33,34, page 19); nothing in the vignette suggests prematurity, and ROP would already have been apparent in earlier normal examinations, which the history rules out.\n\nCorneal degeneration, option D, would produce an anterior corneal opacity rather than a true retro-pupillary leukocoria; the lecture's leukocoria differential is built entirely around lens- and retina-level causes (congenital cataract, retinoblastoma, ROP, Coats disease, PFV, Toxoplasma, Toxocara, retinal dysplasia) and does not include corneal degeneration among them (L33,34, page 19), and it also has no established link to acute trauma in a previously normal eye (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Sudden leukocoria (white pupil) appearing after trauma with prior normal vision is likely traumatic cataract. Retinoblastoma is excluded since vision was normal before trauma.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise post-traumatic (blunt) cataract as a cause of delayed leukocoria in a child with a prior normal eye examination",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2114-2115" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-47", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-peds",
  stem:"A baby about 39 days old presents with excessive lacrimation and photophobia. He has large and hazy cornea in both eyes. His lacrimal system is normal. What is the probable diagnosis?",
  options:["Congenital glaucoma.","Megalocornea","Keratoconus.","Hunter’s syndrome"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Congenital glaucoma is the only condition that combines photophobia, tearing, and a hazy, enlarged cornea in a newborn. The lecture lists these as classic symptoms: \"Photophobia, watering, blepharospasm… Large eye\" (L12, page 35) and the associated signs of \"Corneal oedema, Haab’s stria, Enlarged corneal diameter, Broad limbus, Increased IOP\" (L12, page 39). The combination of a normal lacrimal system with bilateral corneal edema indicates raised intra‑ocular pressure stretching the cornea, which is the pathogenesis described for congenital glaucoma (L12, page 32). Megalocornea presents with a large corneal diameter but the cornea remains clear and there is no photophobia or IOP elevation; this is not described in the lecture and contradicts the presence of corneal haze (not taken from the course material). Keratoconus is an ectatic disorder seen in older children and adolescents, producing a thinned, protruding cornea and irregular astigmatism, not a hazy enlarged cornea in a 39‑day‑old infant (not taken from the course material). Hunter’s syndrome can cause corneal clouding, but it is a systemic mucopolysaccharidosis with other features and does not produce the acute photophobia and corneal edema seen here (not taken from the course material). Therefore option A is the correct diagnosis.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Photophobia, tearing, and a large hazy cornea in infancy are classic signs of congenital glaucoma due to increased IOP stretching the cornea. Early diagnosis prevents optic nerve damage.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the characteristic clinical features of congenital glaucoma in an infant.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2116-2117" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-48", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"A middle aged female with recurrent joint pain gave past history of recurrent attacks of pain, redness and dropped vision in one eye. Recently she complained of similar attack. The most important diagnostic sign of activity is:",
  options:["Festooned pupil","Aqueous flare and cells (iridocyclitis)","Pigmented KPs","Patches of iris atrophy"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Aqueous flare and cells are the correct sign of activity because the lecture lists them as the defining clinical sign of anterior uveitis on its own signs slide: “SLIDE 16 — Anterior uveitis: Signs — cells, flare and hypopyon,” printed as “Anterior chamber: Cells & flare ± hypopyon” (L13,14, slide 16). This woman's story of recurrent joint pain with recurrent attacks of pain, redness and dropped vision in one eye is a recurrent anterior uveitis, most plausibly linked to a seronegative or connective-tissue arthropathy, and the presence of protein (flare) and inflammatory cells in the aqueous is what tells the examiner the process is currently active rather than quiescent, because active inflammatory exudation is what produces them.\n\nFestooned pupil, option A, is not listed among the lecture's active signs; a festooned or irregularly scalloped pupil margin is the visible result of posterior synechiae, which the lecture places among the sequelae and complications of uveitis rather than among the signs of current activity (L13,14, slide 18 area, \"Sequelae and complications — Posterior synechiae\"). A festooned pupil therefore reflects damage already done by a past attack, not proof that inflammation is happening now.\n\nPigmented KPs, option C, are keratic precipitates on the corneal endothelium; the lecture lists them as a sign, but old, pigmented (\"mutton-fat\" type when granulomatous, or simply pigmented and inactive) KPs indicate a healed or chronic low-grade process rather than fresh activity, unlike fine white cells actively deposited during an acute flare — pigmentation implies the deposit has been present long enough to become pigmented, which argues for a resolving rather than an acutely active process (not taken from the course material for the pigmentation-implies-inactivity inference specifically, though KPs themselves are on the lecture's signs slide).\n\nPatches of iris atrophy, option D, are a structural scar left behind after inflammation has damaged the iris stroma; like the festooned pupil, this is a sequela of past uveitis rather than a marker of an inflammatory episode happening at the time of examination, so it cannot be used to certify current activity.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Active uveitis (iridocyclitis) shows inflammatory cells and protein in the anterior chamber (“flare”). These indicate current activity requiring anti-inflammatory treatment.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify aqueous flare and cells as the clinical sign of active anterior uveitis, as distinct from its structural sequelae",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2118-2119" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-49", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-peds",
  stem:"A one-month old baby is brought with complaints of photophobia and watering. Clinical examination shows normal tear passages and clear but large cornea. The most likely diagnosis is:",
  options:["Congenital dacryocystitis","Interstitial keratitis","Keratoconus","Buphthalmos"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"A one‑month‑old infant who has photophobia, watering and a clear but markedly enlarged cornea fits the classic description of buphthalmos, the infantile manifestation of congenital glaucoma. The lecture lists the hallmark symptoms of congenital/developmental glaucoma as \"Photophobia, watering, blepharospasm\" and notes the presence of a \"Large eye\" (L12, page 35). It also describes the ocular signs of this disorder, including an \"enlarged corneal diameter\" and a \"broad limbus\" (L12, page 39). These features arise because elevated intra‑ocular pressure in the immature sclera causes globe enlargement, a process explicitly outlined in the pathogenesis slide (L12, page 32). Therefore option D correctly identifies the diagnosis.\n\nCongenital dacryocystitis (option A) presents with epiphora and discharge due to obstruction of the nasolacrimal duct, but it does not produce corneal enlargement or photophobia, and the tear passages are noted as normal in the stem, contradicting this diagnosis (not taken from the course material).\n\nInterstitial keratitis (option B) is characterized by stromal infiltrates, vascularisation and corneal opacities, not a clear, enlarged cornea; photophobia may occur, but the cornea is not clear nor enlarged (not taken from the course material).\n\nKeratoconus (option C) is a progressive ectasia seen in older children or adolescents, leading to a conical, thinned cornea rather than an enlarged clear cornea; it never presents in a one‑month‑old infant (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> An enlarged, clear cornea in a one-month-old with photophobia suggests buphthalmos, the infant form of congenital glaucoma. The enlarged eye results from raised IOP before scleral maturation.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the ocular condition in an infant with photophobia, watering and an enlarged clear cornea.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2120-2121" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-50", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-glauc",
  stem:"You have been referred a case of open angle glaucoma. Which of the following would be an important point in diagnosing the case?",
  options:["Shallow anterior chamber","Optic disc cupping","Narrow angle","visual acuity and refractive error"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Optic disc cupping is the defining structural sign of open‑angle glaucoma. The lecture states that glaucoma is a triad of elevated intraocular pressure, optic nerve affection (cupping), and visual field defects (L11, page 5). It further emphasizes that clinically one sees an enlargement of the cup‑to‑disc ratio greater than 0.3 (L11, page 13). This makes option B the correct answer because cupping directly reflects the optic neuropathy that characterises the disease. \n\nOption A, shallow anterior chamber, is incorrect. Open‑angle glaucoma is defined by an open, deep anterior chamber; a shallow chamber is typical of angle‑closure glaucoma, which the lecture differentiates on the basis of gonioscopic findings (L11, page 8). \n\nOption C, narrow angle, is also wrong for the same reason: a narrow or closed angle denotes angle‑closure glaucoma, not the open‑angle form being asked about (L11, page 8). \n\nOption D, visual acuity and refractive error, is not a primary diagnostic criterion. The lecture notes that visual acuity may only decline in end‑stage disease and that refractive error is a predisposing factor rather than a diagnostic marker (L11, pages 11 and 14). Hence it does not serve as an important point for diagnosing open‑angle glaucoma (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In open-angle glaucoma, optic disc cupping is a hallmark of progressive optic nerve damage due to increased IOP, even when the anterior chamber remains deep and open.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify optic disc cupping as the key diagnostic sign of open‑angle glaucoma.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2122-2123" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-51", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"On examining the fundus of one patient, multifocal yellowish grey patches with well demarcated borders were seen. Also there are other well defined areas of chorioretinal atrophy with pigmented borders. Which one of the following is the most probable diagnosis for such presentation?",
  options:["Choroiditis","Exudative retinal detachment","Retinitis pigmentosa","Retinal vasculitis"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Choroiditis is the keyed diagnosis because the lecture defines posterior uveitis in exactly these terms on its classification slide: “SLIDE 12 — Uveitis: anatomical classification... 3. Posterior uveitis: (Choroiditis – Chorioretinitis) — Inflammation of the choroid with or without inflammation of the retina” (L13,14, slide 12). Multifocal yellowish-grey patches with well-demarcated borders are the appearance of active choroidal inflammatory foci, and areas of chorioretinal atrophy with pigmented borders alongside them are the scarred, healed lesions left by earlier episodes of the same inflammatory process — a mixture of active and healed lesions is the classic picture of a recurrent or multifocal choroiditis.\n\nExudative retinal detachment, option B, is a separation of the neurosensory retina from the retinal pigment epithelium caused by fluid accumulating beneath it, typically from a choroidal or vascular process; it does not itself present as multifocal yellowish-grey chorioretinal patches with pigmented, well-demarcated atrophic borders — those atrophic, demarcated scars are a feature of resolved focal inflammation, not of a detachment, which instead produces a bullous, mobile retinal elevation (not taken from the course material).\n\nRetinitis pigmentosa, option C, is a progressive, usually bilateral hereditary photoreceptor dystrophy classically presenting with bone-spicule pigmentation, attenuated vessels and waxy disc pallor, a pattern that is diffuse and symmetric rather than multifocal patches with discrete atrophic borders; it does not match the described pattern of discrete yellowish-grey lesions plus separate pigmented-border atrophic patches (not taken from the course material).\n\nRetinal vasculitis, option D, is inflammation centred on the retinal vessels themselves, producing perivascular sheathing, haemorrhages and vascular leakage rather than the described well-demarcated yellowish-grey choroidal patches and pigmented atrophic scars; the fundus picture in the stem centres on choroidal/chorioretinal lesions, not on vessel sheathing, so it points away from a primarily vascular process (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Multifocal yellow-gray lesions with pigmented borders on the fundus indicate choroiditis, an inflammatory condition of the choroid and retina. These can lead to scarring and atrophy.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise multifocal chorioretinal lesions with pigmented atrophic scarring as choroiditis (posterior uveitis)",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2124-2125" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-52", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-va",
  stem:"Visual acuity of a 2-year-old child can be tested by:",
  options:["Snellen’s chart","Landolt’s chart","Optokinetic drum","Electroretinogram"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Optokinetic drum testing is the keyed answer for a 2-year-old because it does not require the child to name letters or point: the examiner watches for reflex following (nystagmus-like) eye and head movements as a rotating striped drum passes in front of the child, which works in a preverbal toddler who cannot cooperate with a chart-reading task. This specific method is not printed in the lecture material (not taken from the course material); the lecture's acuity-testing content is built entirely around the standard adult/cooperative-patient tools — the Snellen chart, the Landolt-C chart and the tumbling-E chart on slide 113 — and the deck explicitly contains no content on paediatric or preverbal acuity testing methods, with the transcription noting zero mentions of children, infants or illiterate-patient charts anywhere in the file (L1,2, slide 113 area).\n\nSnellen's chart, option A, requires the patient to read printed letters aloud and report line by line, which demands literacy and verbal cooperation that a 2-year-old does not have; the lecture presents it as the standard literate-adult acuity chart (L1,2, slide 113), not as a paediatric tool.\n\nLandolt's chart, option B, substitutes a broken ring (\"C\") whose gap direction the patient must indicate instead of naming a letter, which removes the literacy requirement but still needs the child to understand and perform a matching or pointing task; the lecture lists it alongside Snellen's chart as one of the three standard acuity charts without describing any adaptation for toddlers who cannot yet follow such instructions (L1,2, slide 113).\n\nElectroretinogram, option D, is an electrophysiological test of retinal function (photoreceptor and inner retinal responses) rather than a measure of visual acuity; it can detect retinal disease even in an uncooperative or preverbal patient, but it does not quantify how well the child sees, so it is not a visual acuity test at all (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> For toddlers, visual acuity can be assessed using an optokinetic drum, which tests their ability to track moving stripes. It’s suitable for preverbal children.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify optokinetic drum testing as an acuity assessment method usable in a preverbal 2-year-old, as distinct from chart-based tests that need cooperation or verbal response",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2126-2127" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-53", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-va",
  stem:"In pinhole test, the visual acuity gets better if the patient has:",
  options:["Nuclear cataract","Error of refraction","Optic nerve lesion","Macular scar"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Error of refraction is the correct answer because the lecture states the reading rule directly on its pinhole-test slide: “SLIDE 119 — Pinhole Test (how to read the result)... If VA improves, then the visual impairment is due to refractive error (should be correctable with spectacles or a new prescription). If VA worsens, another ocular pathology is causing the visual impairment” (L1,2, slide 119). The pinhole occluder narrows the pencil of light entering the eye to a single ray, which removes the blur caused by an uncorrected refractive error and lets the retina form a sharper image without the need for a lens correction; the preceding slide explains this mechanically: “The use of the pinhole reduces the need to focus light entering the eye” (L1,2, slide 118).\n\nNuclear cataract, option A, is an opacity within the lens substance itself; because the pinhole narrows the beam but does not remove or bypass a cloudy lens, light passing through a nuclear cataract is still scattered by the opacity, so acuity through a pinhole in a cataractous eye characteristically does not improve and may even worsen — matching the lecture's rule that no improvement (or worsening) points to \"another ocular pathology\" rather than to refractive error (L1,2, slide 119).\n\nOptic nerve lesion, option C, causes vision loss from impaired neural transmission rather than from an optical focusing problem; narrowing the pencil of light with a pinhole does nothing to improve conduction along a damaged optic nerve, so acuity fails to improve, again matching the lecture's \"other ocular pathology\" outcome rather than the refractive-error outcome (L1,2, slide 119).\n\nMacular scar, option D, is a structural retinal lesion at the point of central fixation; sharpening the retinal image with a pinhole cannot restore function to photoreceptors destroyed by scarring, so vision through the pinhole remains poor, again falling under the lecture's \"VA worsens/does not improve — other pathology\" category rather than under refractive error (L1,2, slide 119).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Improvement of vision with a pinhole test indicates refractive error, as the pinhole eliminates peripheral light rays and improves focus on the retina.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"State that improvement of visual acuity with the pinhole test indicates uncorrected refractive error, as opposed to an organic ocular lesion",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2128-2129" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-54", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cornea",
  stem:"Photophobia can be due to:",
  options:["Immature senile cataract","Corneal ulcer","Retinal detachment","Senile macular degeneration"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Corneal ulcer is the keyed cause of photophobia because the lecture lists it as a direct symptom of bacterial keratitis (which produces a corneal ulcer): “Bacterial Keratitis: Clinical Features & Investigations... Symptoms: Pain, photophobia, blurred vision, mucopurulent discharge – acute onset” (L15,16, page 8). The cornea is densely innervated, and an epithelial defect with underlying stromal inflammation irritates these nerve endings and triggers reflex ciliary spasm, which is experienced as an abnormal sensitivity to light. The companion Red Eye lecture makes the same mechanism explicit for corneal/anterior-segment inflammation generally: “Photophobia is an abnormal sensitivity to light that accompanies iridocyclitis, either alone or secondary to corneal inflammation. Patients with conjunctivitis have normal light sensitivity” (L37, page 20).\n\nImmature senile cataract, option A, is a lens opacity that reduces and blurs vision through light scatter, but the lens has no sensory innervation of its own and a cataract does not irritate corneal nerves or provoke ciliary spasm, so it is not a recognised cause of photophobia (not taken from the course material).\n\nRetinal detachment, option C, is a posterior-segment disease affecting the neurosensory retina; it can cause photopsia (flashes of light) and field loss, but it does not inflame or irritate the richly innervated anterior structures (cornea, iris, ciliary body) that generate the photophobia response, so it is not a cause of true photophobia (not taken from the course material).\n\nSenile macular degeneration, option D, affects the central retina at the macula and causes distorted or reduced central vision; like retinal detachment, it is a posterior-segment process with no effect on corneal or iridociliary innervation, and it is not listed among photophobia-producing conditions (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Photophobia occurs when the cornea is inflamed or ulcerated due to irritation of corneal nerves. It’s not seen in retinal or lens conditions.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify corneal ulcer (via bacterial keratitis) as a cause of photophobia, as distinct from posterior-segment and lens conditions that do not irritate anterior-segment nerves",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2130-2131" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-55", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cornea-surg",
  stem:"Measuring corneal curvature is done using:",
  options:["Pachymetry","Corneal topography","Gonioscopy","Biometry"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Corneal topography is the keyed answer because the lecture names it directly as the tool for mapping corneal shape and curvature: screening for corneal ectasia (a disease of abnormal curvature) is described as \"MANDATORY before refractive surgery (corneal topography)\" (L15,16, page 21), and the keratoconus diagnosis section states its topographic sign explicitly — \"Topography: inferior steepening, asymmetric bowtie pattern\" (L15,16, page 23). Topography works by mapping the anterior corneal surface point by point and displaying the resulting curvature as a colour-coded map, which is exactly what is needed to detect the irregular, steepened curvature of a diseased or ectatic cornea.\n\nPachymetry, option A, measures corneal thickness, not curvature; it is used alongside topography in refractive-surgery and ectasia screening (for example to confirm an adequate residual stromal bed), but it reports a single thickness value rather than a shape or curvature map, so it cannot substitute for topography when curvature specifically is the question (not taken from the course material for pachymetry's definition, though the lecture uses it as a companion screening test).\n\nGonioscopy, option C, examines the anterior chamber angle using a mirrored contact lens to assess structures such as the trabecular meshwork; it does not image or measure the anterior corneal curvature at all, and is used for angle and glaucoma assessment rather than for corneal shape (not taken from the course material).\n\nBiometry, option D, measures ocular dimensions — principally axial length and (via keratometry, a related but distinct measurement) average corneal power — mainly to calculate intraocular lens power before cataract surgery; it yields a single average curvature/power value rather than the detailed point-by-point curvature map that topography provides, so it is not the tool used to characterise corneal curvature in detail (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Corneal topography maps the curvature and shape of the cornea, useful for diagnosing keratoconus or planning refractive surgery.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify corneal topography as the investigation that maps corneal curvature, as used in keratoconus diagnosis and pre-refractive-surgery screening",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2132-2133" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-57", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-glauc",
  stem:"Applanation tonometry:",
  options:["Can be performed using a Schiotz tonometer","Must be performed on a sitting patient","Is inaccurate on eyes with abnormal scleral rigidity","Is the most accurate method of measuring intraocular pressure"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Applanation tonometry is regarded as the reference standard for measuring intraocular pressure because it directly quantifies the force required to flatten a small area of the cornea, eliminating the influence of corneal curvature and thickness to a greater extent than other methods (not taken from the course material). This is why option D, stating that applanation tonometry is the most accurate method of measuring intraocular pressure, is the keyed correct answer. The lecture on glaucoma emphasizes that elevated intraocular pressure is a central diagnostic criterion (L11, slide 5) but does not discuss the specific techniques used to obtain that measurement, so the answer relies on general ophthalmic knowledge rather than the provided slides.\n\nOption A is incorrect because a Schiotz tonometer works by indentation, not applanation; it applies a known weight to depress the cornea rather than flattening it (not taken from the course material). Option B is wrong because applanation tonometry can be performed with the patient seated or supine; the posture is not a prerequisite for the technique (not taken from the course material). Option C is inaccurate because while very low scleral rigidity can affect some tonometers, the Goldmann applanation method is calibrated to account for typical variations in ocular rigidity and remains reliable in most abnormal eyes (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Applanation tonometry (e.g., Goldmann) directly measures the force required to flatten a small corneal area, making it the most accurate method for IOP measurement.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the most accurate method for measuring intraocular pressure.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2136-2137" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-58", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-trauma",
  stem:"A 29 years old mechanic was hammering an iron object when he felt something hitting his right eye. On eye examination by the slit lamp, a sealed entry wound was seen in the right temporal cornea otherwise the rest of the anterior segment was free. What investigation is not advised in this condition?",
  options:["CT eye and orbit","Plain X ray to the orbit","MRI","B scan ultrasound"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"MRI is the correct \"not advised\" investigation because the lecture states this as a core rule when a metallic intraocular foreign body is suspected: “SLIDE 52 — If you suspect IOFB ★ CORE... DO NOT DO MRI SCAN IF A METALLIC IOFB IS SUSPECTED” (L25, slide 52). A mechanic hammering iron who feels something strike the eye, with a sealed entry wound in the cornea, is the classic setup for a retained metallic intraocular foreign body from a high-velocity metal fragment; the strong magnetic field of an MRI scanner can move or heat a ferromagnetic fragment lodged inside the globe, causing further mechanical damage to intraocular structures.\n\nCT eye and orbit, option A, is explicitly recommended instead of MRI in this scenario: the same slide lists \"CT SCAN — Determine the location and presence of a metallic IOFB [and] show the shape and integrity of the traumatized globe\" (L25, slide 52), so it is an appropriate, advised investigation, not a contraindicated one.\n\nPlain X-ray to the orbit, option B, is likewise listed as an appropriate investigation on the same slide — \"Investigation may include: Plain film x-rays of the head\" (L25, slide 52) — and plain films have long been used to screen for radio-opaque metallic foreign bodies, so it is advised rather than avoided.\n\nB-scan ultrasound, option D, is also listed among the useful investigations on that slide (\"Ultrasound may be useful\", L25, slide 52), and the accompanying figures show a B-scan ultrasound image of an IOFB (L25, slide 53/54); it is generally used cautiously (avoiding pressure on an open globe) but is not the investigation the lecture singles out as prohibited — that designation is reserved for MRI alone.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> MRI is contraindicated if a metallic intraocular foreign body is suspected, as the magnetic field can move or heat the metal, causing serious damage.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recall that MRI is contraindicated when a metallic intraocular foreign body is suspected, unlike CT, plain X-ray or B-scan ultrasound",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2138-2139" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-59", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-dr",
  stem:"In the management of diabetic retinopathy, optimal glycemic control is targeted to achieve which of the following HbA 1 c levels?",
  options:["5%","6%","7%","8%"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"7% is the keyed HbA1c target because tight glycemic control is the lecture's stated strategy for limiting diabetic retinopathy: \"intensive glycemic control is associated with a reduced risk of newly diagnosed retinopathy and reduced progression of existing retinopathy in people with IDDM\" and, for type 2 disease, \"intensive glycemic control compared to conventional treatment was associated with reduction in progression to severe nonproliferative and proliferative retinopathy, a decreased incidence of macular edema, and reduction in need for panretinal and focal macular laser photocoagulation\" (L23). The lecture establishes that keeping blood glucose control tight reduces the onset and progression of retinopathy, but the specific numeric target of an HbA1c around 7% is not itself printed in this deck (not taken from the course material); it is the widely used general diabetes-control target that corresponds to \"intensive\" or \"tight\" glycemic control as the lecture describes it.\n\n5%, option A, is below the normal non-diabetic reference range for HbA1c and is not an achievable or recommended treatment target for a person with diabetes; setting a target this low would risk dangerous hypoglycaemia rather than meaningfully reducing retinopathy risk beyond what tight control at a realistic target already achieves (not taken from the course material).\n\n6%, option B, is close to the non-diabetic/near-normal range and is tighter than the standard target used in most diabetic patients; the lecture's own framing is about \"intensive\" versus \"conventional\" control achieving benefit, and the conventionally accepted intensive target sits at approximately 7%, not 6%, in routine practice (not taken from the course material).\n\n8%, option D, corresponds to looser, \"conventional\" rather than \"intensive\" glycemic control; the lecture states that conventional control (as opposed to intensive control) was associated with more progression to severe retinopathy and more need for laser treatment, so a target this high would not represent the level of control the lecture credits with reducing retinopathy risk (not taken from the course material for the specific percentage, though the concept of conventional versus intensive control being less protective is stated in the lecture).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Optimal glycemic control in diabetic retinopathy aims for HbA1c around 7%, reducing the risk of progression and other microvascular complications.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"State the target HbA1c level for optimal glycemic control in diabetic retinopathy management",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2140-2141" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-66", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-squint",
  stem:"Corneal light reflex testing was performed on a patient. The light reflection was seen in the center of the right eye and in the temporal part of the pupil of the left eye. Which of the following is the significance of these findings?",
  options:["15 degree left esotropia","15 degree left exotropia","45 degree left esotropia","45 degree left exotropia"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"15 degree left esotropia is the bank's printed key. The lecture's own worked photograph of the corneal light reflex test supports a temporally-displaced reflex meaning the eye is turned inward, not outward: on the cover-test slide, the fixing eye has its reflex centred, while the deviating eye is described as \"TURNED IN toward the nose: its iris is displaced nasally, a wide white crescent of sclera is exposed on its TEMPORAL side, and its corneal light reflex is displaced OUTWARD, sitting toward the temporal edge of the pupil rather than centrally... a manifest convergent deviation — ESOTROPIA\" (L29, slide 11). The stem describes exactly this pattern for the left eye — reflex in the temporal part of the pupil, with the right eye's reflex central — so by the lecture's own example this is a left esotropia, matching the printed key of A. The lecture also states plainly that the millimetre-to-degree conversion used to size a deviation from reflex displacement is never printed anywhere in this deck (L29, note on slides 5/11/18), so the specific figure of 15 degrees for this amount of displacement is not taken from the course material.\n\n15 degree left exotropia, option B, describes the correct magnitude class of deviation but the wrong direction: exotropia requires the deviating eye's reflex to be displaced nasally (toward the inner canthus), which is the mirror image of what the lecture's slide 6 diagram shows for exotropia — \"the eye ... is turned OUT ... its iris sits against the outer (temporal) corner and the wide white crescent is on the NASAL side\" (L29, slide 6) — not the temporal-reflex pattern given in the stem.\n\n45 degree left esotropia, option C, has the correct direction but an unsupported magnitude; the lecture never gives a millimetre-to-degree conversion scale, so no specific angular value, including 45 degrees, can be derived from the stem's description of reflex position alone (not taken from the course material).\n\n45 degree left exotropia, option D, is wrong on both counts: it inverts the direction (exotropia would need a nasally displaced reflex, not the temporal one described) and assigns an unsupported magnitude for the same reason as option C.\n\nDiscrepancy note: the page marks A (15 degree left esotropia) as the key, but its own printed explanation box reasons toward exotropia, stating \"A temporal light reflex means the eye is deviated outward (exotropia)\". This directly contradicts the lecture's own worked example, in which a temporally displaced corneal light reflex is used to demonstrate esotropia, not exotropia. Per standing rule, the printed key (A) is not disputed or moved; the box's stated reasoning is recorded here as a discrepancy only, without a ruling on which physiological direction convention the box's authors intended.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A temporal light reflex means the eye is deviated outward (exotropia). Each millimeter of displacement roughly equals 15 degrees of deviation.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Interpret a temporally displaced corneal light reflex on Hirschberg-style testing as esotropia, matching the lecture's own worked example",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2153-2154" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-67", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-refract",
  stem:"Which of the following is the most common cause of vision impairment?",
  options:["Cataract","Gluacoma","Uncorrected refractive errors","Trachoma"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Uncorrected refractive errors are the correct answer to \"most common cause of vision impairment\": myopia, hyperopia and astigmatism are extremely common in the general population, and when they are left uncorrected they blur vision without destroying the eye's structure, which is why they outnumber all disease-based causes of visual impairment worldwide combined. This global epidemiological ranking is not stated anywhere in the ophthalmology lecture material searched for this course (not taken from the course material); the lectures on refractive errors define emmetropia and ametropia and describe their optics, but they do not rank refractive error against other causes of vision impairment by prevalence.\n\nCataract, option A, is a major cause of vision impairment and, in many epidemiological rankings, the leading cause of blindness specifically, which the cataract lecture does print (\"Leading cause of reversible blindness worldwide\", L18, page 4) (as opposed to vision impairment in general, a broader category that also includes milder degrees of visual loss); it is not the single most common cause of vision impairment overall once uncorrected refractive error, which is far more prevalent and generally milder, is counted (not taken from the course material).\n\nGlaucoma, option B, is a major cause of irreversible blindness worldwide because of progressive optic nerve damage, which the chronic-visual-loss lecture prints as \"Leading cause of irreversible blindness\" (L35,36, page 5), but it is far less prevalent in the general population than uncorrected refractive error and is not ranked as the most common cause of vision impairment overall (not taken from the course material).\n\nTrachoma, option D, is an infectious cause of corneal scarring and blindness that remains endemic in some regions, but it affects a much smaller proportion of the global population than uncorrected refractive error and is not the leading cause of vision impairment worldwide (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Globally, the most common cause of visual impairment is uncorrected refractive error (myopia, hyperopia, astigmatism).\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"State that uncorrected refractive error is the most common cause of vision impairment worldwide",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2155-2156" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-76", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-orbit",
  stem:"Following ocular trauma, a patient presented with enophthalmos and anesthesia of lower eyelid, cheek, side of nose, upper lip, upper teeth and gums. Which of the following is the nerve that is most probably involved in the trauma and caused these manifestations?",
  options:["Supraorbital nerve","Infraorbital nerve","Facial nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The patient’s enophthalmos and loss of sensation over the lower eyelid, cheek, lateral nose, upper lip, and upper teeth is classic for an orbital‑floor (blow‑out) fracture that compresses the infra‑orbital canal. The deck states that \"INFRAORBITAL NERVE ANAESTHESIA: Involving the lower lid, cheek, side of nose, upper lip, upper teeth and gums if the fracture involves the infraorbital canal\" (L25, slide 22). Enophthalmos is listed as a possible sign of an orbital‑floor fracture (L25, slide 22). Therefore the nerve most likely injured is the infra‑orbital nerve, option B.\n\nThe supra‑orbital nerve supplies the forehead, scalp and upper eyelid; it does not innervate the lower lid, cheek, or upper lip, so it cannot explain the described anesthesia pattern (not taken from the course material).\n\nThe facial nerve is a motor nerve that controls the muscles of facial expression and does not provide cutaneous sensation to the mid‑facial region; thus it would not cause the sensory deficits noted (not taken from the course material).\n\nThe glossopharyngeal nerve carries taste and general sensation from the posterior third of the tongue, pharynx, and tonsils, and has no branches to the orbit or mid‑face, making it an implausible cause of the presented signs (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Orbital floor fractures commonly injure the infraorbital nerve, causing numbness of the lower eyelid, cheek, and upper lip.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the nerve damaged in an orbital floor fracture that produces infra‑orbital sensory loss and enophthalmos",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2173-2174" }
];
